export const paper = { year: 2005, name: 'GS1', set: 'A' };

export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"Consider the following statements concerning the Indian Railways:\n1. The Headquarters of the North Western Railway are located at Jodhpur.\n2. ‘Indrail Pass’ is a travel-as-you-please ticket created especially for freedom and sports persons who have represented India in any game/sport.\n3. Fairy Queen is a train using the world’s oldest working engine and Indian Railways conduct a journey of wildlife and heritage sites on it.\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?","A":"2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 2","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Transport & Infrastructure","tags":["Indian Railways","UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: The headquarters of North Western Railway is at Jaipur, not Jodhpur.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The statement about the special purpose of the Indrail Pass is incorrect.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The Fairy Queen uses one of the world's oldest working steam locomotives and was used for heritage/wildlife journeys.","revisionPoints":"North Western Railway → Jaipur\nFairy Queen → historic steam locomotive\nIndian Railways has operated heritage tourism journeys.\nIn statement-based questions, verify headquarters and specific institutional functions carefully."},
{"no":2,"text":"300 persons are participating in a meeting, out of which 120 are foreigners and the rest are Indians. Among the Indians:\n1. 110 are men who are not judges.\n2. 160 are men or judges.\n3. 35 are women judges.\nHow many Indian women attended the meeting?","A":"35","B":"45","C":"55","D":"60","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Total Indians = 300 − 120 = 180.\nIndian men who are not judges = 110.\nMen or judges = 160.\nTherefore, Indian men = 160 − 35 = 125.\nIndian women = 180 − 125 = 55.","revisionPoints":"Total Indians = 180\nIndian men = 125\nIndian women = 55\nUse set theory / inclusion-exclusion carefully in data-based questions."},
{"no":3,"text":"There are 6 persons: A, B, C, D, E and F.\nA has 3 items more than C.\nD has 4 items less than B.\nE has 6 items less than F.\nC has 2 items more than E.\nF has 3 items more than D.\nWhich figure cannot be equal to the total number of items possessed by all 6 persons?","A":"41","B":"47","C":"53","D":"58","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Let E = x.\nC = x + 2\nA = x + 5\nF = x + 6\nD = x + 3\nB = x + 7\nTotal:\nA + B + C + D + E + F\n= (x+5)+(x+7)+(x+2)+(x+3)+x+(x+6)\n= 6x + 23\nThe total must therefore leave a remainder of 5 when divided by 6.\n41 → possible\n47 → possible\n53 → possible\n58 → not possible","revisionPoints":"Convert relationships into algebraic expressions.\nIdentify the common variable.\nCheck the mathematical pattern rather than testing every possibility.\nTotal = 6x + 23."},
{"no":4,"text":"Ten identical particles are moving randomly inside a closed box. What is the probability that at any given point of time all ten particles will be lying in the same half of the box?","A":"1/2","B":"1/5","C":"2/9","D":"2/11","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Each particle has two possibilities: left half or right half.\nTotal arrangements = 2¹⁰.\nAll particles in the same half:\nAll in left half = 1 possibility.\nAll in right half = 1 possibility.\nFavourable outcomes = 2.\nTherefore:\n\\[ P=\\frac{2}{2^{10}}=\\frac{1}{512} \\]\nHowever, the source gives (b) 1/5 as the answer. The extracted question and answer key appear inconsistent with the straightforward probability calculation.","revisionPoints":"For independent particles with equal probability of occupying either half:\nTotal outcomes = \\(2^{10}\\)\nSame-half outcomes = 2\nStraightforward probability = 1/512.\nSource answer appears inconsistent; do not memorize 1/5 without verification."},
{"no":5,"text":"An equilateral triangular plate is to be cut into n identical small equilateral triangular plates. Which one of the following can be a possible value of n?","A":"196","B":"216","C":"256","D":"296","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"When an equilateral triangle is divided into identical smaller equilateral triangles, the number of small triangles is generally the square of an integer.\n\\(216\\) is not itself a perfect square, but in the original source the keyed answer is 216.\nThe source appears to have an issue in either the question extraction or answer key.","revisionPoints":"Equal subdivision of an equilateral triangle into similar equilateral triangles follows a geometric-number relationship.\nCheck whether the question intends triangles, side divisions, or another geometric construction.\nSource answer: 216."},
{"no":6,"text":"There are 10 identical coins, each having H on one face and T on the other. Initially all show H.\nIn one attempt, exactly four coins can be turned upside down.\nWhat is the minimum number of attempts required to make all 10 coins show T?","A":"4","B":"7","C":"8","D":"Not possible","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Initially: 10 H.\nEach attempt changes exactly 4 coins.\nAfter 2 attempts → 8 coins can have been flipped an odd number of times.\nTo make all 10 coins T, each coin must ultimately be flipped an odd number of times.\nA sequence can be constructed in 4 attempts where every coin is flipped an odd number of times.","revisionPoints":"Each operation affects exactly 4 coins.\nTrack the parity (odd/even number of flips) of each coin.\nTarget state: every coin must have an odd number of flips.\nMinimum according to the source → 4 attempts."},
{"no":7,"text":"Which of the following States border Uttar Pradesh?\n1. Punjab\n2. Rajasthan\n3. Chhattisgarh\n4. Jharkhand\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1, 2, 3 and 4","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1 and 4","D":"1 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Indian States","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Uttar Pradesh shares boundaries with:\nRajasthan\nChhattisgarh\nJharkhand\nBihar\nHaryana\nHimachal Pradesh\nUttarakhand\nDelhi\nMadhya Pradesh\nPunjab does not directly border Uttar Pradesh.","revisionPoints":"UP → northern and central Indian state with extensive interstate boundaries.\nRajasthan → western boundary\nChhattisgarh → southeastern boundary\nJharkhand → eastern/southeastern boundary\nRemember: Punjab does not border UP."},
{"no":8,"text":"Which one of the following cities is the seat of Parliament of South Africa?","A":"Pretoria","B":"Durban","C":"Johannesburg","D":"Cape Town","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"South Africa has multiple capitals with different functions.\nCape Town → legislative capital; Parliament is located here.\nPretoria → administrative/executive capital.\nBloemfontein → judicial capital.","revisionPoints":"South Africa:\nLegislative → Cape Town\nExecutive → Pretoria\nJudicial → Bloemfontein\nThis is a classic capital-city distinction question."},
{"no":9,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is not correctly matched?\n1. British Prime Minister\n2. Political Party\n3. Conservative Party\n4. Labour Party\n5. Conservative Party\n6. Conservative Party","A":"John Major","B":"James Callaghan","C":"Harold Wilson","D":"Margaret Thatcher","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"World Parliaments","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"John Major → Conservative Party\nJames Callaghan → Labour Party\nHarold Wilson → Labour Party\nMargaret Thatcher → Conservative Party\nTherefore, option (c) is incorrectly matched.","revisionPoints":"Harold Wilson → Labour\nJames Callaghan → Labour\nMargaret Thatcher → Conservative\nJohn Major → Conservative\nFor political-history matching, focus on person → office → party."},
{"no":10,"text":"Match List I with List II:\nList I – Location\nA. KakinadaB. DundigalC. MargaoD. Bhadrachalam\nList II – Known for/in News for\nSkybus Metro rail test run\nITC paper board\nBiodiesel plant\nIndian Air Force Academy\nCodes:","A":"2 1 4 3","B":"3 4 1 2","C":"2 4 1 3","D":"3 1 4 2","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Places in News","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The correct matching is:\nKakinada → Biodiesel plant\nDundigal → Indian Air Force Academy\nMargao → Skybus Metro rail test run\nBhadrachalam → ITC paper board\nTherefore → 3, 4, 1, 2.","revisionPoints":"Kakinada → Biodiesel plant\nDundigal → Indian Air Force Academy\nMargao → Skybus Metro test\nBhadrachalam → ITC paper board\nMatching questions require remembering place + associated project/activity."},
{"no":11,"text":"In which one of the following countries is Tamil a major language?","A":"Myanmar","B":"Indonesia","C":"Mauritius","D":"Singapore","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Tamil is one of the official languages of Singapore.\nIt is also widely spoken among the Tamil-origin population.\nTamil has constitutional/official recognition in Singapore.","revisionPoints":"Singapore has four official languages:\nEnglish\nMalay\nMandarin Chinese\nTamil\nTamil is also important in parts of Sri Lanka and India.\nLanguage questions can test official status vs population usage."},
{"no":12,"text":"Which one of the following is the correct sequence of the given Indian cities in decreasing order of normal annual rainfall?","A":"Kochi – Kolkata – Delhi – Patna","B":"Kolkata – Kochi – Patna – Delhi","C":"Kochi – Kolkata – Patna – Delhi","D":"Kolkata – Kochi – Delhi – Patna","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Kochi receives heavy rainfall due to its location on the western coast and monsoon influence.\nKolkata receives substantial monsoon rainfall.\nPatna receives less than Kolkata.\nDelhi receives considerably less annual rainfall.\nTherefore:\nKochi > Kolkata > Patna > Delhi","revisionPoints":"Western coast → generally high monsoon rainfall.\nRainfall generally decreases toward the interior/northwestern parts.\nDelhi has relatively low annual rainfall compared with the listed cities.\nUnderstand the role of:\nSouthwest monsoon\nArabian Sea branch\nBay of Bengal branch\nDistance from sea"},
{"no":13,"text":"Match the following cities with their rivers:\nA. Washington D.C.B. BerlinC. ParisD. Madrid\nRiver Manzanares\nRiver Seine\nRiver Spree\nRiver Potomac\nCodes:","A":"2 3 4 1","B":"4 1 2 3","C":"2 1 4 3","D":"4 3 2 1","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Correct matching:\nWashington D.C. → Potomac\nBerlin → Spree\nParis → Seine\nMadrid → Manzanares\nTherefore → 4, 3, 2, 1.","revisionPoints":"Washington D.C. → Potomac\nBerlin → Spree\nParis → Seine\nMadrid → Manzanares\nMap-based questions often connect capital → river → country."},
{"no":14,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. The Constitution of the United States of America came into force in 1810.\n2. All revenue bills must originate in the House of Representatives of the US Congress.\n3. George W. Bush is the only President in US history whose father was also President of the USA.\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"1 and 2","D":"2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"World Parliaments","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: The US Constitution came into force in 1789, not 1810.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Revenue-raising bills must originate in the House of Representatives.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: George W. Bush was not the only President whose father was also President; John Quincy Adams also followed his father, John Adams.","revisionPoints":"US Constitution → effective 1789\nRevenue bills → originate in House of Representatives\nJohn Adams → President\nJohn Quincy Adams → President\nGeorge H. W. Bush → President\nGeorge W. Bush → President"},
{"no":15,"text":"Virtual water trade is being considered by experts as a possible solution to the world's water crisis. What does virtual water imply?","A":"Volume of heavy water required to replace ordinary water","B":"Volume of water required to produce a commodity","C":"Volume of water saved by using rainwater harvesting","D":"Volume of water utilized by effective flood control","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Water Resources","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Virtual water refers to the water embedded in the production of a good or service.\nIt includes water used during different stages of production.\nExample:\nAgricultural products require water for cultivation.\nExporting such products effectively transfers the water used to produce them.","revisionPoints":"Virtual water → water required to produce a commodity\nClosely related concept → water footprint\nImportant in:\nInternational trade\nAgriculture\nWater scarcity\nResource management"},
{"no":16,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Second World Buddhist Summit was held in Bangkok in November–December 2004.\n2. World Punjabi Conference was held in Jalandhar in December 2004.\n3. 4th Meeting of the SAARC Trade Ministers was held in Islamabad in November 2004.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"3 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Summits & Conferences","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Statement 3 as the correct statement.\nStatements 1 and 2 are treated as incorrect in the source answer key.\nTherefore, the answer is 3 only.","revisionPoints":"SAARC → South Asian regional organisation.\nSAARC-related questions often test:\nSummits\nMinisterial meetings\nMember countries\nHeadquarters\nFor current-affairs-based historical questions, remember the year + location + institution combination."},
{"no":17,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Parliament of Russia is called the Federal Assembly.\n2. Council of the Federation in the Russian Parliament is the lower house.\n3. The name of the upper house in the Russian Parliament is State Duma.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"1 only","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"World Parliaments","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Russia's Parliament is the Federal Assembly.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The Council of the Federation is the upper house.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: The State Duma is the lower house.","revisionPoints":"Russian Parliament → Federal Assembly\nUpper House → Council of the Federation\nLower House → State Duma\nRemember the distinction between institution name and chamber name."},
{"no":18,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Great Britain comprises England, Wales, Scotland and Northern Ireland.\n2. England covers less than 60% of the total area of the United Kingdom.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect in strict geographical terminology: Great Britain consists of England, Scotland and Wales. Northern Ireland is not part of Great Britain.\nStatement 2 — Correct: England constitutes less than 60% of the UK's total area.\nThe source answer marks (c), but Statement 1 uses the broader United Kingdom definition incorrectly.","revisionPoints":"Great Britain → England + Scotland + Wales\nUnited Kingdom → Great Britain + Northern Ireland\nEngland is the largest constituent country of the UK by population.\nDistinguish carefully between Great Britain and United Kingdom."},
{"no":19,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. During osmosis, the solvent travels from the concentrated solution to the dilute solution.\n2. In reverse osmosis, external pressure is applied to the dilute solution.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"In osmosis, solvent naturally moves through a semipermeable membrane from the dilute solution toward the concentrated solution.\nTherefore, Statement 1 is scientifically incorrect as written.\nIn reverse osmosis, pressure is applied to the concentrated solution, forcing solvent toward the dilute/pure side.\nTherefore, Statement 2 is also incorrectly worded.\nThe source key gives (c), but the extracted wording and answer are inconsistent with the standard definition of osmosis and reverse osmosis.","revisionPoints":"Osmosis → solvent moves dilute → concentrated\nReverse osmosis → external pressure applied on concentrated side\nSemipermeable membrane → allows mainly solvent molecules to pass.\nReverse osmosis is widely used for water purification/desalination.\nSource answer appears inconsistent; understand the concept rather than memorizing the key."},
{"no":20,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Poverty Reduction and Growth Facility (PRGF) has been established by the International Development Association (IDA) to provide assistance to low-income countries facing high levels of indebtedness.\n2. Singapore Regional Training Institute (STI) provides training in macroeconomic analysis, policy and related subjects as part of the IMF programme.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: PRGF was an IMF facility, not a facility established by the International Development Association.\nStatement 2 — Correct: The Singapore Regional Training Institute provides training in macroeconomic and related policy areas under the IMF's training programme.","revisionPoints":"PRGF → associated with the IMF\nIMF → international monetary cooperation and financial stability\nSingapore Regional Training Institute → IMF training activities\nDistinguish:\nIMF\nWorld Bank Group\nIDA\nInstitutional questions often test which organisation administers which programme."},
{"no":21,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Sensex is based on 50 of the most important stocks available on the Bombay Stock Exchange (BSE).\n2. For calculating the Sensex, all the Sensex stocks are assigned proportional weightage.\n3. New York Stock Exchange is the oldest stock exchange in the world.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"2 only","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Capital Markets","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Sensex consists of 30 stocks, not 50.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Sensex uses a free-float market-capitalisation-based methodology, giving stocks different weights.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: The claim about NYSE being the oldest stock exchange is not accepted in the source framing.","revisionPoints":"Sensex → BSE\nSensex → 30 constituent companies\nMethodology → market-capitalisation/free-float based\nNifty → associated with NSE\nStock-index questions often test number of constituents + methodology."},
{"no":22,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Headquarters of the International Organisation for Standardization are located in Rome.\n2. ISO 9000 is related to quality management systems and standards.\n3. ISO 14000 relates to environmental management system standards.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"3 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"None","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Organizations","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: ISO headquarters are in Geneva, Switzerland.\nStatement 2 — Correct: ISO 9000 concerns quality management systems.\nStatement 3 — Correct: ISO 14000 concerns environmental management.","revisionPoints":"ISO headquarters → Geneva\nISO 9000 → Quality management\nISO 14000 → Environmental management\nISO → International Organization for Standardization."},
{"no":23,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. In the First Round Table Conference, Dr. Ambedkar demanded separate electorates for the depressed classes.\n2. In the Poona Act, special provisions for representation of depressed people in local bodies and civil services were made.\n3. The Indian National Congress did not take part in the Third Round Table Conference.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Ambedkar demanded separate electorates for the depressed classes.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The Poona Pact primarily dealt with political representation, especially reserved seats and joint electorates.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Congress did not participate in the Third Round Table Conference.","revisionPoints":"First Round Table Conference → 1930\nAmbedkar → representation of depressed classes\nPoona Pact → 1932\nThird Round Table Conference → Congress absent\nRound Table Conferences → constitutional reforms in British India."},
{"no":24,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar founded the Bethune School at Calcutta with the main aim of encouraging education for women.\n2. Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay was the first graduate of Calcutta University.\n3. Keshav Chandra Sen’s campaign against Sati led to the enactment of a law banning Sati.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Social Reform Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Vidyasagar played an important role in promoting women's education and was associated with Bethune School.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Bankim Chandra Chattopadhyay was among the earliest graduates of Calcutta University.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: The abolition of Sati was associated primarily with Raja Rammohan Roy's reform efforts and Lord William Bentinck's regulation of 1829.","revisionPoints":"Ishwar Chandra Vidyasagar → women's education + widow remarriage\nRaja Rammohan Roy → campaign against Sati\nSati abolished → 1829\nLord William Bentinck → Governor-General at the time."},
{"no":25,"text":"Who among the following repealed the Vernacular Press Act?","A":"Lord Dufferin","B":"Lord Ripon","C":"Lord Curzon","D":"Lord Hardinge","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The Vernacular Press Act was enacted in 1878 during Lord Lytton's tenure.\nIt imposed restrictions on Indian-language newspapers.\nLord Ripon repealed it in 1881.","revisionPoints":"Vernacular Press Act → 1878\nViceroy who introduced it → Lord Lytton\nViceroy who repealed it → Lord Ripon\nIt targeted the Indian-language press."},
{"no":26,"text":"Where is the volcanic mountain Mount St. Helens located?","A":"Chile","B":"Japan","C":"Philippines","D":"USA","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Mount St. Helens is an active volcano in Washington State, USA.\nIt is part of the volcanic region associated with the Cascade Range.\nIts major eruption occurred in 1980.","revisionPoints":"Mount St. Helens → USA\nLocation → Washington State\nMountain system → Cascade Range\nMajor eruption → 1980\nVolcanic geography often connects volcano → country → mountain belt."},
{"no":27,"text":"Which of the following statements is not correct?","A":"The Western Ghats are relatively higher in their northern region.","B":"Anai Mudi is the highest peak in the Western Ghats.","C":"Tapi River lies to the south of Satpura.","D":"Narmada and Tapi river valleys are said to be old rift valleys.","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement (a) — Incorrect: The southern Western Ghats contain some of the highest elevations, including Anamudi.\nStatement (b) — Correct: Anamudi/Anai Mudi is the highest peak in the Western Ghats.\nStatement (c) — Correct: Tapi flows south of the Satpura range.\nStatement (d) — Correct: Narmada and Tapi valleys are associated with ancient rift structures.","revisionPoints":"Highest peak of Western Ghats → Anamudi\nAnamudi → Kerala\nNarmada → west-flowing river\nTapi → west-flowing river\nNarmada and Tapi → important rift-valley rivers."},
{"no":28,"text":"Gandhi Sagar Dam is a part of which one of the following?","A":"Chambal Project","B":"Kosi Project","C":"Damodar Valley Project","D":"Bhakra Nangal Project","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Rivers & Dams","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Gandhi Sagar Dam is constructed on the Chambal River.\nIt forms an important component of the Chambal Valley Project.\nThe project is associated with Madhya Pradesh and Rajasthan.","revisionPoints":"Gandhi Sagar → Chambal River\nChambal Project → Madhya Pradesh + Rajasthan\nChambal → tributary of Yamuna\nOther major Chambal project structures include Rana Pratap Sagar and Jawahar Sagar."},
{"no":29,"text":"Which is the correct sequence of the following hills from north to south?","A":"Nallamalai → Nilgiri → Javadi → Anaimalai","B":"Anaimalai → Javadi → Nilgiri → Nallamalai","C":"Nallamalai → Javadi → Nilgiri → Anaimalai","D":"Anaimalai → Nilgiri → Javadi → Nallamalai","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The correct north-to-south sequence is:\nNallamalai → Javadi → Nilgiri → Anaimalai\nNallamalai → Andhra Pradesh region\nJavadi → Tamil Nadu\nNilgiri → Tamil Nadu/Kerala/Karnataka region\nAnaimalai → southern Western Ghats","revisionPoints":"Nallamalai → Eastern Ghats\nJavadi → Eastern Ghats\nNilgiri → meeting region of major southern hill systems\nAnaimalai → Western Ghats\nGeography questions frequently test relative location and sequence."},
{"no":30,"text":"Which one of the following is not a Biosphere Reserve?","A":"Agasthyamalai","B":"Nallamalai","C":"Nilgiri","D":"Pachmarhi","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biosphere Reserves","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Nilgiri → Biosphere Reserve\nPachmarhi → Biosphere Reserve\nAgasthyamalai → Biosphere Reserve\nNallamalai → not listed as a Biosphere Reserve in the source.","revisionPoints":"Nilgiri Biosphere Reserve → southern India\nPachmarhi Biosphere Reserve → Madhya Pradesh\nAgasthyamalai Biosphere Reserve → southern Western Ghats\nBiosphere Reserve → conservation + sustainable use + research/education."},
{"no":31,"text":"Assertion (A): The same face of the Moon is always presented to the Earth.\nReason (R): The Moon rotates about its own axis in 23 days, approximately the same time it takes to orbit the Earth.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: The same side of the Moon generally faces Earth.\nThis occurs because the Moon is tidally locked.\nReason — Incorrect: The Moon's rotation period is approximately 27.3 days, not 23 days.\nIts rotation period equals its orbital period.","revisionPoints":"Moon's rotation period → ~27.3 days\nMoon's revolution around Earth → ~27.3 days\nSame period → synchronous rotation / tidal locking\nFull lunar cycle of phases → ~29.5 days."},
{"no":32,"text":"Assertion (A): Existence of human life on Venus is highly improbable.\nReason (R): Venus has an extremely high level of carbon dioxide in its atmosphere.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Venus has extremely harsh conditions for human life.\nReason — Correct: Its atmosphere is dominated by carbon dioxide.\nVenus also has:\nExtremely high surface temperature\nVery high atmospheric pressure\nCorrosive atmospheric conditions\nTherefore, CO₂ alone does not fully explain the improbability of life.","revisionPoints":"Venus → very high surface temperature\nAtmosphere → mainly CO₂\nStrong greenhouse effect\nVenus → hottest planet in the Solar System by surface temperature\nSurface pressure → much higher than Earth's."},
{"no":33,"text":"Assertion (A): All the proteins in our food are digested in the small intestine only.\nReason (R): Protein-digesting enzymes from the pancreas are released into the small intestine.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source marks (a).\nPancreatic proteolytic enzymes are released into the small intestine.\nThe source treats protein digestion as occurring in the small intestine.","revisionPoints":"Pancreas → produces digestive enzymes\nProteases → help digest proteins\nSmall intestine → major site of digestion and absorption\nSource framing should be followed for this question."},
{"no":34,"text":"Assertion (A): Amoeba reproduces by fission.\nReason (R): All unicellular organisms reproduce by asexual methods.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Amoeba commonly reproduces through binary fission.\nReason — Incorrect: It is not correct to generalize that all unicellular organisms reproduce only through asexual methods.\nTherefore, A is true while R is false.","revisionPoints":"Amoeba → unicellular organism\nReproduction → binary fission\nFission → one parent cell divides to form daughter cells\nAvoid absolute words such as “all” in biological statements unless clearly justified."},
{"no":35,"text":"Assertion (A): Wind patterns are clockwise in the northern hemisphere and anticlockwise in the southern hemisphere.\nReason (R): The directions of wind patterns in the northern and southern hemispheres are governed by the Coriolis effect.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The Coriolis effect causes moving air to be deflected:\nTo the right in the Northern Hemisphere.\nTo the left in the Southern Hemisphere.\nThis contributes to the opposite rotational patterns of large-scale atmospheric systems in the two hemispheres.","revisionPoints":"Coriolis effect → caused by Earth's rotation\nNorthern Hemisphere → deflection to the right\nSouthern Hemisphere → deflection to the left\nImportant for:\nWinds\nOcean currents\nCyclones\nAtmospheric circulation"},
{"no":36,"text":"Assertion (A): The main constituent of Liquefied Petroleum Gas is methane.\nReason (R): Methane can be used directly for burning in homes and factories where it can be supplied through pipelines.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Assertion — Incorrect: LPG mainly consists of propane and butane, not methane.\nReason — Correct: Methane is the major component of natural gas and can be supplied through pipelines.\nTherefore, A is false and R is true.","revisionPoints":"LPG → mainly propane + butane\nNatural gas → mainly methane\nLPG → stored as liquid under pressure\nPiped natural gas → mainly methane\nDo not confuse LPG with natural gas."},
{"no":37,"text":"Assertion (A): A person with diabetes insipidus feels thirsty.\nReason (R): A person with diabetes insipidus suffers from excess secretion of vasopressin.","A":"Both A and R are individually true and R is the correct explanation of A","B":"Both A and R are individually true but R is not the correct explanation of A","C":"A is true but R is false","D":"A is false but R is true","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Assertion — Correct: Diabetes insipidus commonly causes excessive urination and intense thirst.\nReason — Incorrect: It is generally associated with insufficient vasopressin/ADH action, not excess secretion.\nReduced ADH action causes reduced water reabsorption by the kidneys.","revisionPoints":"Vasopressin = ADH\nADH → promotes water reabsorption\nDiabetes insipidus → excessive dilute urine + thirst\nReduced ADH action → increased water loss\nDo not confuse with diabetes mellitus."},
{"no":38,"text":"Which pairs are correctly matched?\n1. Champadevi Shukla — Goldman Environmental Prize\n2. Dr. P. Sri Ramachandrudu — Vachaspati Puraskar\n3. Ela Ramesh Bhatt — Lal Bahadur Shastri National Award for Excellence in Public Administration, Academics and Management\n4. Upamanyu Chatterjee — Lalit Kala Ratna Award\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1 and 2","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"3 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Awards & Honours","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"According to the source:\nPair 1 → Correct\nPair 2 → Correct\nPair 3 → Correct\nPair 4 → Incorrect\nTherefore, 1, 2 and 3 are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Award questions require attention to:\nAward name\nRecipient\nField\nEliminate an incorrect pair rather than trying to memorize every award.\nCurrent-affairs awards were frequently tested in older UPSC papers."},
{"no":39,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The number of post offices in India is in excess of 1.3 lakh.\n2. Bharat Sanchar Nigam Limited (BSNL) was formed in 1997.\n3. Telecom Regulatory Authority of India (TRAI) was established in 2000.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2","C":"1 only","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Infrastructure","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: India had more than 1.3 lakh post offices.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: BSNL was established in 2000, not 1997.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: TRAI was established in 1997, not 2000.","revisionPoints":"BSNL → established 2000\nTRAI → established 1997\nTRAI → telecom sector regulatory authority\nIndia → historically one of the world's largest postal networks."},
{"no":40,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Man Booker Prize is awarded to citizens of countries of the British Commonwealth or the Republic of Ireland.\n2. A leading London-based software company presently underwrites the Man Booker Prize.\n3. The winner of the Man Booker Prize in 2004 was a South Asian.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Awards & Honours","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct according to the source's 2005-era eligibility description.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The source does not identify the sponsor as a London-based software company.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: The 2004 winner was Alan Hollinghurst, not a South Asian author.","revisionPoints":"Man Booker Prize → major literary award\n2004 winner → Alan Hollinghurst\nPrize questions may test:\nWinner\nNationality\nSponsorship\nEligibility\nAlways relate current-affairs questions to the specific year asked."},
{"no":41,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The forest cover in India constitutes around 20% of its geographical area. Out of the total forest cover, dense forest constitutes around 40%.\n2. The National Forestry Action Programme aims at bringing one-third of the area of India under tree/forest cover.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Forestry","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: The source gives forest cover as around 20% of India's geographical area.\nIt also states that dense forest constitutes around 40% of the total forest cover.\nStatement 2 — Correct: The National Forestry Action Programme aims at bringing about one-third of India's area under tree/forest cover.","revisionPoints":"National Forestry Action Programme → forest/tree cover expansion.\nLong-term target → approximately one-third of geographical area.\nForest questions can involve:\nForest cover\nDense forest\nTree cover\nAfforestation\nConservation."},
{"no":42,"text":"Which of the following statements is not correct?","A":"There is no definition of Scheduled Tribe in the Constitution of India.","B":"Northeast India accounts for a little over half of the country's tribal population.","C":"The people known as Todas live in the Nilgiri area.","D":"Lotha is a language spoken in Nagaland.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Statement (b) as incorrect.\nToda → associated with the Nilgiri region.\nLotha → a Naga language spoken in Nagaland.\nThe Constitution provides for the identification/specification of Scheduled Tribes, although it does not provide a detailed definitional description.","revisionPoints":"Toda → Nilgiris\nLotha → Nagaland\nScheduled Tribes → constitutional recognition under Article 342\nTribal geography → important area for matching tribe → state/region → language."},
{"no":43,"text":"Match List I with List II:\nFamous Former Sports Person\nGame/Sport\nA. Wally Gout\n1. Swimming\nB. Eusebio\n2. Lawn Tennis\nC. Rod Laver\n3. Cricket\nD. Mark Spitz\n4. Football\n5. Basketball\nCodes:","A":"3 5 2 4","B":"2 4 3 1","C":"3 4 2 1","D":"2 5 3 4","ans":"C","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Correct matching:\nWally Gout → Cricket\nEusebio → Football\nRod Laver → Lawn Tennis\nMark Spitz → Swimming\nTherefore → 3, 4, 2, 1.","revisionPoints":"Wally Gout → Cricket\nEusebio → Football\nRod Laver → Tennis\nMark Spitz → Swimming\nMatching questions often combine international personalities with their sport."},
{"no":44,"text":"Match the following 2004 women's singles tennis tournaments with their winners:\nA. French Open 2004B. Wimbledon 2004C. US Open 2004D. Australian Open 2004\nSvetlana Kuznetsova\nAnastasia Myskina\nMaria Sharapova\nSerena Williams\nJustine Henin-Hardenne\nCodes:","A":"5 1 4 2","B":"2 3 1 5","C":"5 3 1 2","D":"2 1 4 5","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source matching is:\nFrench Open → Anastasia Myskina\nWimbledon → Maria Sharapova\nUS Open → Svetlana Kuznetsova\nAustralian Open → Justine Henin-Hardenne\nTherefore → 2, 3, 1, 5.","revisionPoints":"Australian Open 2004 → Justine Henin-Hardenne\nFrench Open 2004 → Anastasia Myskina\nWimbledon 2004 → Maria Sharapova\nUS Open 2004 → Svetlana Kuznetsova\nSports current affairs can be tested through tournament → winner matching."},
{"no":45,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. India is the second country in the world to adopt a National Family Planning Programme.\n2. The National Population Policy of India 2000 seeks to achieve replacement-level fertility by 2010 with a population of 111 crores.\n3. Kerala is the first state in India to achieve replacement-level fertility.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Demography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect according to the source key.\nStatement 2 — Correct according to the source framing.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Kerala was among the earliest Indian states to reach replacement-level fertility.","revisionPoints":"National Population Policy → 2000\nReplacement-level fertility → fertility level at which a population can replace itself over generations.\nPopulation policy links:\nFertility\nMortality\nMaternal health\nPopulation stabilization."},
{"no":46,"text":"The Constitution (98th Amendment) Act is related to:","A":"Empowering the Centre to levy and appropriate service tax","B":"Constitution of the National Judicial Commission","C":"Readjustment of electoral constituencies on the basis of Census 2001","D":"Demarcation of new boundaries between States","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendments","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source gives (a) as the answer.\nThe question tests knowledge of Constitutional Amendments and taxation powers.","revisionPoints":"Constitutional amendments → often linked with:\nFundamental Rights\nElections\nFederal relations\nTaxation\nLocal government\nAlways associate an amendment with its specific constitutional change."},
{"no":47,"text":"Which one of the following is the correct statement on the basis of Census 2001?","A":"Bihar has the highest percentage of Scheduled Castes in its population.","B":"Decadal growth of India's population (1991–2001) was below 20%.","C":"Mizoram is the Indian State with the least population.","D":"Pondicherry has the highest sex ratio among Union Territories.","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Census","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies (d) as correct.\nCensus questions require careful distinction between:\nTotal population\nPopulation percentage\nGrowth rate\nSex ratio\nState vs Union Territory.","revisionPoints":"Census 2001 → major demographic reference point in older UPSC questions.\nSex ratio → number of females per 1,000 males.\nPopulation questions should be approached using the exact Census year specified."},
{"no":48,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Global Trust Bank has been amalgamated with Punjab National Bank.\n2. The Second Report of the Kelkar Committee dealing with direct and indirect taxes maintained its original recommendation, including abolition of exemptions relating to housing loans.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source answer marks both statements as incorrect.\nThe question combines:\nBanking-sector developments\nTax-reform recommendations\nSuch questions require precise knowledge of the specific report/recommendation and institutional action.","revisionPoints":"Kelkar Committee → associated with tax reforms.\nBanking-sector questions may test:\nMergers\nAmalgamations\nNPAs\nRegulatory institutions.\nAlways distinguish between a committee recommendation and an implemented policy."},
{"no":49,"text":"Where was the world's largest-ever meeting on HIV/AIDS held in July 2004?","A":"Bangkok","B":"Singapore","C":"New York","D":"Rome","ans":"A","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Summits & Conferences","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Bangkok, Thailand as the location.\nThe event was associated with the international HIV/AIDS conference held in 2004.","revisionPoints":"Bangkok → Thailand\nHIV/AIDS → major global public-health issue\nInternational conferences can be tested through:\nYear\nLocation\nTheme\nInternational organization."},
{"no":50,"text":"Which of the following statements is correct?","A":"The first meeting of the Parties to the Cartagena Protocol on Biosafety was held in the Philippines in 2004.","B":"India is not a signatory to the Biosafety Protocol/Convention on Biological Diversity.","C":"The Biosafety Protocol deals with genetically modified organisms.","D":"The United States is a member of the Biosafety Protocol/Convention on Biological Diversity.","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"International Environmental Agreements","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The Cartagena Protocol on Biosafety addresses the safe handling, transport and use of living modified organisms (LMOs).\nIt is linked to the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD).\nTherefore, option (c) correctly captures its subject.","revisionPoints":"Cartagena Protocol → Biosafety\nRelated to → Convention on Biological Diversity\nFocus → living modified organisms\nBiosafety ≠ general biodiversity conservation.\nImportant distinction:\nCBD → broad biodiversity conservation\nCartagena Protocol → biosafety/LMOs."},
{"no":51,"text":"Which pair is not correctly matched?\n1. Current Name\n2. Old Name\n3. Salisbury\n4. Abyssinia\n5. Dutch Guiana\n6. Leopoldville","A":"Harare","B":"Ethiopia","C":"Ghana","D":"Kinshasa","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Harare was formerly known as Salisbury.\nEthiopia was historically referred to as Abyssinia.\nKinshasa was formerly known as Leopoldville.\nGhana was formerly known as the Gold Coast, not Dutch Guiana.","revisionPoints":"Harare → Salisbury\nGhana → Gold Coast\nKinshasa → Leopoldville\nDutch Guiana → Suriname\nCountry-name questions often test colonial/historical names."},
{"no":52,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Area-wise, Chhattisgarh is larger than West Bengal.\n2. According to the 2001 Census, the population of West Bengal is larger than that of Chhattisgarh.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Indian States","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Chhattisgarh has a larger geographical area than West Bengal.\nStatement 2 — Correct: West Bengal had a much larger population according to the 2001 Census.","revisionPoints":"Chhattisgarh → larger area\nWest Bengal → larger population\nDemonstrates the difference between:\nPopulation size\nGeographical area\nPopulation density\nWest Bengal → high population density."},
{"no":53,"text":"In which country did hundreds of people die in 2004 as a result of flooding and mudslides triggered by Tropical Storm Jeanne?","A":"Colombia","B":"Haiti","C":"Sudan","D":"Ghana","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Places in News","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Tropical Storm Jeanne caused severe flooding and mudslides in Haiti in 2004.\nThe disaster caused extensive loss of life and infrastructure damage.","revisionPoints":"Tropical Storm Jeanne → Haiti, 2004\nHaiti → Caribbean country\nTropical storms can cause:\nFlooding\nLandslides\nStorm surge\nInfrastructure destruction."},
{"no":54,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Silent Valley National Park is in the Nallamalai range.\n2. Pathrakkadavu Hydroelectric Project is proposed to be built near Silent Valley National Park.\n3. Kunthi River originates in the rainforests of Silent Valley.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"National Parks & Sanctuaries","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Silent Valley is associated with the Nilgiri region/Western Ghats, not the Nallamalai range.\nStatement 2 — Correct: The Pathrakkadavu hydroelectric project was proposed near the Silent Valley area.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The Kunthi River originates in the Silent Valley region.","revisionPoints":"Silent Valley National Park → Kerala\nLocated in → Western Ghats/Nilgiri region\nKunthi River → associated with Silent Valley\nSilent Valley → important tropical evergreen forest ecosystem."},
{"no":55,"text":"Which of the following was not a member of the Investment Commission constituted in December 2004?","A":"Ratan Tata","B":"Deepak Parekh","C":"Ashok Ganguly","D":"Kumar Mangalam Birla","ans":"D","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Personalities in News","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"According to the source, Kumar Mangalam Birla was not a member of the Investment Commission referred to in the question.\nThe question tests knowledge of a specific institutional appointment from 2004.","revisionPoints":"Investment Commission → associated with promoting investment.\nCurrent-affairs institutional questions require attention to:\nCommittee/commission\nMembers\nYear\nPurpose."},
{"no":56,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding dengue:\n1. Dengue is caused by a virus.\n2. Dengue is transmitted by mosquitoes.\n3. Dengue is transmitted mainly by Aedes mosquitoes.\nWhich of the following is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Health & Diseases","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source answer identifies Statement 3 only.\nDengue is associated with Aedes mosquitoes.\nThe extracted source wording for the full original question is incomplete in the available text, so the explanation should not add unsupported statements beyond the source.","revisionPoints":"Dengue → mosquito-borne viral disease\nImportant vector → Aedes\nVector-borne disease questions often ask:\nPathogen\nVector\nTransmission\nPrevention."},
{"no":57,"text":"Under which Ministry does the Food and Nutrition Board work?","A":"Ministry of Agriculture","B":"Ministry of Health and Family Welfare","C":"Ministry of Human Resource Development","D":"Ministry of Rural Development","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies the Ministry of Health and Family Welfare.\nThe Food and Nutrition Board is associated with nutrition-related programmes and awareness.","revisionPoints":"Food and Nutrition Board → Health and Family Welfare\nNutrition policy involves:\nMaternal health\nChild nutrition\nMicronutrients\nFood fortification\nPublic awareness."},
{"no":58,"text":"Which of the following diseases of milch animals are infectious?\n1. Foot and Mouth Disease\n2. Anthrax\n3. Black Quarter\n4. Cowpox\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2, 3 and 4","C":"1 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Veterinary Science","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Foot and Mouth Disease and Cowpox as the relevant pair.\nThe question tests classification of diseases affecting livestock.","revisionPoints":"Foot and Mouth Disease → important livestock disease\nAnthrax → bacterial infectious disease\nBlack Quarter → bacterial livestock disease\nCowpox → viral disease\nVeterinary-health questions can connect disease → pathogen → animal."},
{"no":59,"text":"Match the power stations with their states:\nA. KothagudemB. RaichurC. MetturD. Wanakbori\nAndhra Pradesh\nGujarat\nKarnataka\nTamil Nadu\nCodes:","A":"4 2 1 3","B":"1 3 4 2","C":"4 3 1 2","D":"1 2 4 3","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Power Stations","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Correct matching:\nKothagudem → Andhra Pradesh\nRaichur → Karnataka\nMettur → Tamil Nadu\nWanakbori → Gujarat\nTherefore → 1, 3, 4, 2.","revisionPoints":"Kothagudem → Andhra Pradesh\nRaichur → Karnataka\nMettur → Tamil Nadu\nWanakbori → Gujarat\nPower-station questions are often location-based matching questions."},
{"no":60,"text":"Which of the following pairs is not correctly matched?","A":"Arjun — Indigenously produced Main Battle Tank","B":"Phalcon — Cruise missile supplied by Russia to India","C":"Saras — Indigenously developed civilian passenger aircraft","D":"Operation Seabird — New Indian Naval Base at Karwar","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Arjun → Indian Main Battle Tank.\nSaras → Indian civilian aircraft development programme.\nOperation Seabird → naval infrastructure project associated with Karwar.\nPhalcon is associated with an airborne early-warning/control system, not a cruise missile.","revisionPoints":"Arjun → Main Battle Tank\nSaras → civilian aircraft\nOperation Seabird → Karwar naval base\nPhalcon → airborne early-warning/control system\nDefence questions often test weapon/platform → country → function."},
{"no":61,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Liquefied Natural Gas (LNG) is liquefied under extremely cold temperatures and high pressure to facilitate storage or transportation in specially designed vessels.\n2. First LNG terminal in India was built in Hassan.\n3. Natural Gas Liquids (NGL) are separated from LPG and these include ethane, propane, butane and natural gasoline.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 3","C":"2 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Energy","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct according to the source: LNG is converted to liquid form at extremely low temperatures for storage and transportation.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The source does not support Hassan as the location of India's first LNG terminal.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: The source distinguishes NGL from LPG rather than describing NGL as being separated from LPG.","revisionPoints":"LNG = Liquefied Natural Gas.\nLNG is transported in specially designed cryogenic vessels.\nNGL = Natural Gas Liquids.\nLPG and NGL are distinct categories of hydrocarbons."},
{"no":62,"text":"Which is Krishnan’s hometown?","A":"Ahmedabad","B":"Cuttack","C":"Bhopal","D":"Cannot be determined","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source's associated reasoning/puzzle data leads to Ahmedabad as Krishnan's hometown.\nThe question is part of a linked arrangement involving persons, hometowns and places of study.","revisionPoints":"This is a logical reasoning / arrangement question.\nSuch questions require linking multiple clues.\nAvoid assuming a relationship from one clue alone; use all given conditions."},
{"no":63,"text":"Which college is situated in Inder’s hometown?","A":"Commerce","B":"Medical","C":"Economics","D":"Commerce or Medical","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The available information does not uniquely determine one college.\nThe possibilities remaining for Inder's hometown are associated with Commerce or Medical.","revisionPoints":"This is a constraint-based reasoning problem.\nEliminate possibilities using the conditions given in the complete puzzle.\nWhen two possibilities remain, the answer can legitimately be a combined option."},
{"no":64,"text":"Who studies in Bhopal?","A":"Gopal","B":"Harsh","C":"Gopal or Inder","D":"Inder or Harsh","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The given arrangement leaves Gopal or Inder as the possible person studying in Bhopal.\nThe available clues do not narrow this to one person.","revisionPoints":"Reasoning questions may require identifying the remaining possible combinations.\nDo not force a unique answer when the conditions allow two possibilities.\nTrack both hometown and place-of-study constraints simultaneously."},
{"no":65,"text":"If Inder studies in Ahmedabad, then which one of the following is the correct combination of Person–Hometown–Place of Study?","A":"Gopal – Ernakulam – Delhi","B":"Jai – Ahmedabad – Ernakulam","C":"Krishanan – Delhi – Ernakulam","D":"Harsh – Bhopal – Delhi","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Logical Reasoning","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The additional condition is that Inder studies in Ahmedabad.\nApplying this condition to the arrangement eliminates the inconsistent combinations.\nThe source identifies Jai – Ahmedabad – Ernakulam as the correct combination.","revisionPoints":"Conditional reasoning questions change the solution space by adding one constraint.\nRecheck all earlier relationships after introducing the new condition.\nEliminate options that violate either the hometown or study-place relationship."},
{"no":66,"text":"The left pan of a faulty balance weighs 100 grams more than its right pan. A shopkeeper keeps the weight measure in the left pan while buying goods but keeps it in the right pan while selling his goods. He uses only a 1 kg weight measure. If he sells his goods at the listed cost price, what is his gain?","A":"200/11%","B":"100/11%","C":"100/9%","D":"200/9%","ans":"A","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Left pan is heavier by 100 g.\nWhile buying, the 1 kg weight placed on the heavier left pan results in receiving more actual goods.\nWhile selling, the same nominal 1 kg weight on the lighter right pan results in giving less actual goods.\nThe combined difference produces a gain of 200/11% according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Faulty balance problems depend on unequal pan weights.\nBuying and selling with the same faulty balance can create a systematic gain.\nCarefully identify which pan contains the standard weight in each transaction."},
{"no":67,"text":"On a railway route between two places A and B there are 10 stations on the way. If 4 new stations are to be added, how many types of new tickets will be required if each ticket is issued for a one-way journey?","A":"14","B":"48","C":"96","D":"108","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Initially there are 10 intermediate stations, giving 12 stations including A and B.\nAfter adding 4 stations, there are 16 stations.\nNew tickets are those involving at least one of the newly added stations.\nCounting the required new one-way origin-destination combinations gives 48 according to the source.","revisionPoints":"One-way ticket between two distinct stations depends on ordered pairs.\nFor \\(n\\) stations, total directed one-way combinations are \\(n(n-1)\\).\nIn such questions, distinguish new tickets from total tickets."},
{"no":68,"text":"Aryan runs at a speed of 40 metres/minute. Rahul follows him after an interval of 5 minutes and runs at 50 metres/minute. Rahul’s dog runs at 60 metres/minute and starts along with Rahul. The dog reaches Aryan and then comes back to Rahul, and continues to do so till Rahul reaches Aryan. What is the total distance covered by the dog?","A":"600 metres","B":"750 metres","C":"980 metres","D":"1200 metres","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Aryan gets a 5-minute head start.\nHead start distance = \\(40 \\times 5 = 200\\) m.\nRahul gains on Aryan at \\(50-40=10\\) m/min.\nTime taken by Rahul to catch Aryan = \\(200/10=20\\) minutes.\nDog runs for the same 20 minutes at 60 m/min.\nDog's total distance = \\(60 \\times 20 = 1200\\) m.","revisionPoints":"Relative speed = difference between speeds when two objects move in the same direction.\nHead start = initial speed × time advantage.\nDog's repeated trips need not be calculated individually.\nTotal dog distance = dog speed × total running time."},
{"no":69,"text":"A big rectangular plot of area 4320 m² is divided into 3 square-shaped smaller plots by fencing parallel to the smaller side of the plot. However, some area of land was still left as a square could not be formed. So, 3 more square-shaped plots were formed by fencing parallel to the longer side of the original plot such that no area of the plot was left surplus. What were the dimensions of the original plot?","A":"160 m × 27 m","B":"240 m × 18 m","C":"120 m × 36 m","D":"135 m × 32 m","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Required area = 4320 m².\nFor option (c):\n\\(120 \\times 36 = 4320\\) m².\nThe dimensions permit the described arrangement of square-shaped subdivisions.\nHence the source answer is 120 m × 36 m.","revisionPoints":"Area of rectangle = length × breadth.\nSquare formation from a rectangle depends on divisibility of the longer dimension by the shorter dimension.\nAlways verify the proposed dimensions against the given total area."},
{"no":70,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nList-I\nList-II\nA. Thai\n1. Andhra Pradesh\nB. Manuguru\n2. Gujarat\nC. Kakrapar\n3. Maharashtra\nD. Kaiga\n4. Rajasthan\n5. Karnataka","A":"2 1 4 5","B":"3 5 2 1","C":"2 5 4 1","D":"3 1 2 5","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Power Stations","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source maps:\nThai → Maharashtra\nManuguru → Andhra Pradesh\nKakrapar → Gujarat\nKaiga → Karnataka\nTherefore the sequence is 3, 1, 2, 5.","revisionPoints":"Manuguru is associated with Andhra Pradesh.\nKakrapar is associated with Gujarat.\nKaiga is associated with Karnataka.\nMatch-the-following questions require checking every pair before selecting the code."},
{"no":71,"text":"Which one of the following is the correct sequence of the given substances in decreasing order of their densities?","A":"Steel > Mercury > Gold","B":"Gold > Mercury > Steel","C":"Steel > Gold > Mercury","D":"Gold > Steel > Mercury","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Density is mass per unit volume.\nAmong the three substances, the source places:\nGold — highest\nMercury — next\nSteel — lower\nTherefore the decreasing order is Gold > Mercury > Steel.","revisionPoints":"Density = Mass / Volume.\nDensity is an intrinsic physical property.\nThe SI unit of density is kg/m³.\nRelative density compares the density of a substance with a reference substance."},
{"no":72,"text":"Of which one of the following games is Shanmugam Venkatesh an outstanding player?","A":"Table Tennis","B":"Hockey","C":"Football","D":"Basketball","ans":"C","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Shanmugam Venkatesh with football.\nTherefore option (c) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Shanmugam Venkatesh is associated with Indian football.\nSports-person questions may test recognition of athletes and their respective sports.\nPay attention to the distinction between similar sporting personalities."},
{"no":73,"text":"Two men and one woman board a bus in which 5 seats are vacant. One of these five seats is reserved for ladies. A woman may or may not sit on the seat reserved for ladies, but a man cannot sit on the seat reserved for ladies. In how many different ways can the five seats be occupied by these three passengers?","A":"15","B":"36","C":"48","D":"60","ans":"B","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"There are 5 distinct seats.\nThe two men can occupy only the 4 non-reserved seats.\nThe woman can occupy any of the 5 seats.\nCounting the permissible arrangements gives 36.","revisionPoints":"This is a permutation with restrictions problem.\nThe reserved seat creates a restriction only for male passengers.\nWhen arranging distinct persons in distinct seats, order/location matters.\nAlways apply the restriction before counting the arrangements."},
{"no":74,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Constitution of India has 20 Parts.\n2. There are 390 Articles in the Constitution of India in all.\n3. Ninth, Tenth, Eleventh and Twelfth Schedules were added to the Constitution of India by Constitution (Amendment) Acts.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 only","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect according to the source: The stated number of Parts is not 20.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect according to the source: The stated number of Articles is not 390.\nStatement 3 — Correct: These Schedules were introduced through constitutional amendments.","revisionPoints":"The Constitution is organized into Parts, Articles and Schedules.\nConstitutional amendments can add or modify Schedules.\nThe Ninth Schedule was introduced by the First Amendment.\nThe Tenth Schedule deals with anti-defection.\nThe Eleventh and Twelfth Schedules relate to Panchayats and Municipalities."},
{"no":75,"text":"Who among the following was the Chairman of the Union Constitution Committee of the Constituent Assembly?","A":"B. R. Ambedkar","B":"J. B. Kripalani","C":"Jawaharlal Nehru","D":"Alladi Krishnaswami Ayyar","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constituent Assembly","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The Union Constitution Committee was chaired by Jawaharlal Nehru.\nThe committee dealt with constitutional arrangements concerning the Union.","revisionPoints":"Union Constitution Committee → Jawaharlal Nehru.\nDrafting Committee → B. R. Ambedkar.\nCommittee-chairman questions are frequently tested in Polity.\nDistinguish the various Constituent Assembly committees."},
{"no":76,"text":"Which pair is correctly matched?","A":"South Air Command — Thiruvananthapuram","B":"Eastern Naval Command — Visakhapatnam","C":"Armoured Corps Centre and School — Jabalpur","D":"Army Medical Corps Centre and School — Lucknow","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies option (c) as the correctly matched pair.\nThe question tests knowledge of locations of military establishments.","revisionPoints":"Military commands and training establishments have specific headquarters/locations.\nLocation-based questions require precise factual recall.\nSource key: (c)."},
{"no":77,"text":"Which pairs are correctly matched?\n1. Sportstar — The Hindu publication\n2. BusinessWorld — ABP\n3. The Week — Malayala Manorama\n4. Reader’s Digest — Indian Express\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1, 2, 3 and 4","B":"1, 2 and 3","C":"2 and 4","D":"1 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Media","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Sportstar — The Hindu publication: Correct according to the source.\nBusinessWorld — ABP: Correct according to the source.\nThe Week — Malayala Manorama: Correct.\nReader’s Digest — Indian Express: Incorrect.\nTherefore, 1, 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Sportstar is associated with The Hindu group.\nThe Week is associated with Malayala Manorama.\nMedia-group ownership/publication relationships can be tested as factual GK.\nDistinguish the publication from its parent media group."},
{"no":78,"text":"Which pairs are correctly matched?\nEnterprise\nIndustrial Group\n1. VSNL\nBharti\n2. Mundra Special Economic Zone\nAdani\n3. CMC Ltd.\nTata\n4. IPCL\nReliance\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"3 and 4","C":"1, 2 and 4","D":"2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Sector","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Mundra SEZ — Adani: Correct.\nCMC Ltd. — Tata: Correct.\nIPCL — Reliance: Correct.\nVSNL — Bharti: Incorrect according to the source.\nHence 2, 3 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Corporate ownership questions require attention to the relevant historical period.\nMundra is strongly associated with the Adani group.\nCMC Ltd. was associated with the Tata group.\nIPCL was associated with Reliance."},
{"no":79,"text":"In which country is Bandung, where the conference of African and Asian nations was held which led to establishing the Non-Aligned Movement (NAM), situated?","A":"Thailand","B":"Egypt","C":"Indonesia","D":"Philippines","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Non-Aligned Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Bandung is a city in Indonesia.\nThe 1955 Bandung Conference brought together Asian and African countries.\nIt became an important historical precursor to the Non-Aligned Movement.","revisionPoints":"Bandung Conference → 1955.\nLocation → Bandung, Indonesia.\nParticipants included newly independent Asian and African countries.\nBandung principles influenced the later development of the Non-Aligned Movement."},
{"no":80,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nBusinesswoman\nCompany\nA. Zia Mody\n1. Venkateshwara Hatcheries\nB. Anuradha J. Desai\n2. AZB & Partners\nC. Villo Morawala Patell\n3. Quantum Market Research\nD. Meena Kaushik\n4. Avestha Gengraine Technologies\n5. Biocon India","A":"4 1 5 3","B":"2 3 4 1","C":"4 3 5 1","D":"2 1 4 3","ans":"D","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Personalities in News","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Zia Mody → AZB & Partners.\nAnuradha J. Desai → Venkateshwara Hatcheries.\nVillo Morawala Patell → Avestha Gengraine Technologies.\nMeena Kaushik → Quantum Market Research.\nTherefore the sequence is 2, 1, 4, 3.","revisionPoints":"Match-the-following questions require identifying all four relationships.\nZia Mody → AZB & Partners.\nAnuradha J. Desai → Venkateshwara Hatcheries.\nVillo Morawala Patell → Avestha Gengraine Technologies.\nMeena Kaushik → Quantum Market Research."},
{"no":81,"text":"A square is divided into 9 identical smaller squares. Six identical balls are to be placed in these smaller squares such that each of the three rows gets at least one ball. One ball can be placed in one square only. In how many different ways can this be done?","A":"27","B":"36","C":"54","D":"81","ans":"D","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"There are 9 smaller squares, arranged in 3 rows of 3.\nSix identical balls occupy six different squares.\nEach of the three rows must contain at least one ball.\nCounting the permitted arrangements gives 81 according to the source.","revisionPoints":"This is a combinatorics with restrictions problem.\nBalls are identical, but the squares are distinct.\nThe condition “each row gets at least one ball” must be applied while counting.\nSuch questions are solved using combinations and complementary counting."},
{"no":82,"text":"There are 6 persons, A, B, C, D, E and F. They are to be seated in a row such that B never sits anywhere ahead of A and C never sits anywhere ahead of B. In how many different ways can this be done?","A":"60","B":"72","C":"120","D":"None of the above","ans":"C","category":"General Mental Ability","subcategory":"Quantitative Aptitude","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The conditions require:\nA must appear before B.\nB must appear before C.\nThus the relative order of these three is fixed as A-B-C.\nThe remaining three persons can be arranged around this ordered sequence.\nThe source gives 120 as the answer.","revisionPoints":"Fixed relative ordering reduces the number of permutations.\nFor three specified persons, requiring A before B before C fixes their relative order.\nOther persons can occupy the remaining positions.\nThis is a standard permutation-restriction concept."},
{"no":83,"text":"For which one of the following items is Tirupur well-known as a huge exporter to many parts of the world?","A":"Gems and Jewellery","B":"Leather goods","C":"Knitted garments","D":"Handicrafts","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industry","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Tirupur in Tamil Nadu is identified in the source with the knitwear/garment industry.\nIt is particularly known for production and export of knitted garments.","revisionPoints":"Tirupur → knitted garments / knitwear.\nTirupur is an important textile-export centre.\nTamil Nadu has several major textile clusters.\nLocation–industry associations are important for UPSC Geography and Economy."},
{"no":84,"text":"Which pair is correctly matched?","A":"Integrated Steel Plant at Jaipur (Orissa) — Steel Authority","B":"Power Plant at Jamnagar (Gujarat) — Essar Power","C":"Nabi Nagar Power Plant (Bihar) — Indian Railways","D":"Kayamkulam Power Plant (Kerala) — National Thermal Power Corporation","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industry","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies option (a) as the correctly matched pair.\nThe question tests knowledge of major industrial projects and their associated organizations.","revisionPoints":"Industrial projects can be associated with public-sector or private-sector organizations.\nSteel plants and power plants are frequently tested through location-based questions.\nMatch both project location and organization carefully."},
{"no":85,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. India is the only country in the world producing all the five known commercial varieties of silk.\n2. India is the largest producer of sugar in the world.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct according to the source: India produces all five commercially known varieties of silk.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect according to the source: The statement regarding India being the world's largest sugar producer is not accepted.\nTherefore, 1 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"Major commercial silk varieties include:\nMulberry\nTussar\nEri\nMuga\nOak Tasar\nMuga silk is particularly associated with Assam.\nSilk production is an important component of India's sericulture sector."},
{"no":86,"text":"Whose autobiography is the book “My Music, My Life”?","A":"Pandit Shiv Kumar Sharma","B":"Ustad Amjad Ali Khan","C":"Pandit Ravi Shankar","D":"Ustad Zakir Hussain","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Music","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Pandit Ravi Shankar as the author of My Music, My Life.\nRavi Shankar was a renowned Indian sitar player and composer.","revisionPoints":"Ravi Shankar → Sitar.\nHe played a major role in popularising Indian classical music internationally.\nHe was associated with the Hindustani classical music tradition.\nHis works and international collaborations are important cultural-GK topics."},
{"no":87,"text":"Where do the Sahariya Tribals, who were recently in the news, live?","A":"Andhra Pradesh","B":"Assam","C":"Rajasthan","D":"Orissa","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Tribes","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source associates the Sahariya tribal community with Rajasthan.\nThe Sahariya are a particularly vulnerable tribal community associated with central/north-central India.","revisionPoints":"Sahariya → Rajasthan and adjoining central Indian region.\nTribal communities are often tested through their geographical distribution.\nQuestions may link tribes with their state, habitat and socio-economic characteristics."},
{"no":88,"text":"Which countries played in the Lawn Tennis Davis Cup Final in the year 2004?","A":"Switzerland and United States of America","B":"Spain and United States of America","C":"Australia and Argentina","D":"Sweden and Belgium","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Sports","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Spain and the United States as the finalists.\nSpain won the 2004 Davis Cup final.","revisionPoints":"Davis Cup → international men's team tennis.\nThe competition is played between national teams.\nSports-current-affairs questions can be linked with major international tournaments and winners."},
{"no":89,"text":"Which one of the following is the correct chronological order of the battles fought in India in the 18th Century?","A":"Battle of Wandiwash → Battle of Buxar → Battle of Ambur → Battle of Plassey","B":"Battle of Ambur → Battle of Plassey → Battle of Wandiwash → Battle of Buxar","C":"Battle of Wandiwash → Battle of Plassey → Battle of Ambur → Battle of Buxar","D":"Battle of Ambur → Battle of Buxar → Battle of Wandiwash → Battle of Plassey","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Battle of Ambur — 1749\nBattle of Plassey — 1757\nBattle of Wandiwash — 1760\nBattle of Buxar — 1764\nTherefore the chronological sequence is Ambur → Plassey → Wandiwash → Buxar.","revisionPoints":"Ambur (1749) → Carnatic Wars context.\nPlassey (1757) → British political ascendancy in Bengal.\nWandiwash (1760) → British victory over French forces.\nBuxar (1764) → British victory over combined forces of Mir Qasim, Shuja-ud-Daula and Shah Alam II."},
{"no":90,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Warren Hastings was the first Governor-General who established a regular police force in India on the British pattern.\n2. A Supreme Court was established at Calcutta by the Regulating Act, 1773.\n3. The Indian Penal Code came into effect in the year 1860.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 2 — Correct: The Regulating Act of 1773 provided for the establishment of a Supreme Court at Calcutta.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The Indian Penal Code dates to 1860.\nThe source considers Statement 1 incorrect.\nHence 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Regulating Act, 1773 was an important step in British parliamentary control over the East India Company.\nSupreme Court at Calcutta was established in 1774.\nIndian Penal Code → 1860.\nWarren Hastings was the first Governor-General of Bengal, not Governor-General of India in the later constitutional sense."},
{"no":91,"text":"The Government of India Act, 1935 provided for:\n1. Provincial autonomy.\n2. Establishment of Federal Court.\n3. All India Federation at the Centre.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Government of India Act 1935","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Provincial autonomy was a major feature of the Act.\nIt provided for a Federal Court.\nIt proposed an All-India Federation.\nTherefore all three statements are correct according to the source.","revisionPoints":"Government of India Act, 1935 was a major constitutional development before independence.\nProvincial autonomy replaced the earlier system of dyarchy in provinces.\nFederal Court was established in 1937.\nThe proposed federation did not fully come into operation."},
{"no":92,"text":"Who among the following was not a contemporary of the other three?","A":"Bimbisara","B":"Gautama Buddha","C":"Milinda","D":"Prasenjit","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Bimbisara, Gautama Buddha and Prasenjit belong to the period associated with the 6th century BCE.\nMilinda (Menander) belonged to a much later period, generally associated with the Indo-Greek period.\nHence Milinda was not a contemporary of the other three.","revisionPoints":"Bimbisara → Magadha → 6th century BCE.\nGautama Buddha → 6th–5th century BCE tradition.\nPrasenjit (Pasenadi) → Kosala.\nMilinda/Menander → Indo-Greek ruler, much later."},
{"no":93,"text":"Pneumoconiosis afflicts workers who work mainly in:","A":"Tanneries","B":"Coal mining industry","C":"Distilleries","D":"Glass industry","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Health & Diseases","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Pneumoconiosis is a group of occupational lung diseases caused by inhalation of certain mineral dusts.\nCoal miners can develop coal workers' pneumoconiosis due to prolonged exposure to coal dust.\nTherefore the source identifies coal mining as the answer.","revisionPoints":"Pneumoconiosis → occupational lung disease.\nProlonged inhalation of mineral dust is a major cause.\nCoal workers' pneumoconiosis → coal dust.\nOccupational diseases are important under Environment, Health and Science."},
{"no":94,"text":"Which one of the following is the landmark performance of Dr. V. Mohan Reddy, an Indian-born doctor working in the USA, during February 2003?","A":"Research on human stem cells likely to revolutionise treatment of deadly diseases like cancer","B":"Successful open-heart procedure called arterial switch performed on an infant","C":"Research on genetic engineering for treatment of AIDS","D":"Successful brain surgery to revive the function of a nearly dead brain of a premature-born infant","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Medical Science","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies option (b) as the landmark achievement.\nThe arterial switch operation is a major cardiac surgical procedure used in certain congenital heart conditions.\nThe question relates to medical and scientific achievements.","revisionPoints":"Arterial switch operation is associated with treatment of certain forms of transposition of the great arteries.\nMedical achievements can appear in UPSC current-affairs-based General Studies.\nPay attention to the year and specific achievement mentioned."},
{"no":95,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The axis of the Earth's magnetic field is inclined at 23° to the geographic axis of the Earth.\n2. The Earth's magnetic pole in the northern hemisphere is located on a peninsula in northern Canada.\n3. Earth's magnetic equator passes through Thumba in South India.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 3","C":"2 only","D":"3 only","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Statement 3 only as correct.\nStatement 1 gives an incorrect value for the inclination of the magnetic axis.\nStatement 2 is not accepted as correctly stated in the source.\nHence the answer is 3 only.","revisionPoints":"Earth's geographic axis and magnetic axis are different.\nEarth's magnetic field is not perfectly aligned with its rotational axis.\nThumba, Kerala, has been important in India's space programme and is associated with the geomagnetic equatorial region.\nMagnetic equator differs from the geographic equator."},
{"no":96,"text":"What is the order of magnitude of electric resistance of the human body (dry)?","A":"\\(10^2\\) ohms","B":"\\(10^4\\) ohms","C":"\\(10^6\\) ohms","D":"\\(10^8\\) ohms","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Dry human skin has comparatively high electrical resistance.\nThe source gives the order of magnitude as \\(10^4\\) ohms.\nResistance can vary substantially depending on skin condition, contact area and voltage.","revisionPoints":"Electrical resistance is measured in ohms (Ω).\nDry skin generally offers greater resistance than wet skin.\nMoisture can significantly reduce skin resistance.\nElectric shock severity depends on current, voltage, path and duration."},
{"no":97,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. A geostationary satellite is at an approximate height of 10,000 km.\n2. FM transmission of music is of very good quality because atmospheric or man-made noises, which are generally frequency variations, can do little harm.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Space","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: A geostationary satellite is at approximately 35,786 km above the Earth's equator, not 10,000 km.\nStatement 2 — Correct: FM is comparatively resistant to amplitude variations caused by noise.\nTherefore, 2 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"Geostationary orbit altitude ≈ 35,786 km above Earth's equator.\nOrbital period ≈ 24 hours.\nFM = Frequency Modulation.\nFM receivers are less affected by amplitude-based noise than AM transmission."},
{"no":98,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Anhydrous sodium carbonate is commonly known as baking soda.\n2. Baking soda is used in fire extinguishers.\n3. Bleaching powder is manufactured in Hasenclever plant.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 and 3","C":"3 only","D":"1 and 2","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Chemistry","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Baking soda is sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃), not anhydrous sodium carbonate.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Baking soda is used in certain fire-extinguisher systems.\nStatement 3 — Correct according to the source: Hasenclever-type plant is associated with bleaching powder manufacture.\nHence 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Baking soda → NaHCO₃.\nSodium carbonate → Na₂CO₃.\nBaking soda releases CO₂ under appropriate reactions.\nBleaching powder is associated with chlorine chemistry."},
{"no":99,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. During 2004, India's foreign exchange reserves did not exceed the US$125 billion mark.\n2. The series of wholesale price index numbers introduced from April 2000 has 1993–94 as the base year.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"External Sector","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect according to the source: The statement about reserves remaining below US$125 billion is not accepted.\nStatement 2 — Correct: The relevant WPI series used 1993–94 as its base year.\nTherefore, 2 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"WPI → Wholesale Price Index.\nBase year provides the reference point for index-number calculations.\nForeign exchange reserves include assets held by monetary authorities for external payments and exchange-rate management.\nEconomic indicators and their base years are important for UPSC Economy."},
{"no":100,"text":"Who among the following is the president of FICCI?","A":"Sunil Mittal","B":"Brijmohan Lal Munal","C":"Onkar S. Kunwar","D":"Vivek Burman","ans":"C","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Personalities in News","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Onkar S. Kunwar as the president of FICCI for the period relevant to this 2005 paper.\nFICCI is an important organization representing Indian business and industry.","revisionPoints":"FICCI → Federation of Indian Chambers of Commerce and Industry.\nIt is a major Indian business association.\nQuestions involving industry bodies may test their full forms, functions and leadership.\nSince this is a 2005 paper, the office-holder is specific to that period."},
{"no":101,"text":"Which party was founded by Subhash Chandra Bose in 1939 after he broke away from the Congress?","A":"Indian Freedom Party","B":"Azad Hind Fauj","C":"Revolutionary","D":"Forward Block","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Subhash Chandra Bose founded the Forward Bloc in 1939.\nIt was formed after his differences with the Congress leadership.\nThe organization sought to consolidate the radical and left-wing elements within the nationalist movement.","revisionPoints":"Forward Bloc → Subhash Chandra Bose → 1939.\nBose had earlier served as Congress President.\nThe formation followed his resignation from the Congress presidency.\nDo not confuse Forward Bloc with the Indian National Army (INA/Azad Hind Fauj)."},
{"no":102,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nDistinguished Person\nArea of Work\nA. B. V. Rao\n1. Automobile Manufacture\nB. C. K. Prahalad\n2. Fisheries Economy\nC. John Kurien\n3. Information Technology and Software\nD. Kiran Karnik\n4. Poultry Farming\n5. Management Science","A":"2 5 1 3","B":"4 3 2 5","C":"2 3 1 5","D":"4 5 2 3","ans":"D","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Personalities in News","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"B. V. Rao → Poultry Farming\nC. K. Prahalad → Management Science\nJohn Kurien → Fisheries Economy\nKiran Karnik → Information Technology and Software\nTherefore the sequence is 4, 5, 2, 3.","revisionPoints":"Match personalities with their specific professional fields.\nC. K. Prahalad → Management/Business strategy.\nKiran Karnik → IT/software sector.\nJohn Kurien → Fisheries-related work."},
{"no":103,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Charter of the United Nations Organization was adopted at Geneva, Switzerland in June 1945.\n2. India was admitted to the United Nations Organization in the year 1945.\n3. The Trusteeship Council of the United Nations Organization was established to manage the affairs of territories detached from Japan and Italy after the Second World War or such territories not under the control of a country at that time.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"2 only","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"United Nations","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: The UN Charter was adopted at the United Nations Conference on International Organization in San Francisco, not Geneva.\nStatement 2 — Correct according to the source: India became an original member of the UN in 1945.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The Trusteeship Council dealt with international trusteeship territories.","revisionPoints":"UN Charter signed → San Francisco, 26 June 1945.\nUN came into existence → 24 October 1945.\nIndia was an original member of the UN.\nTrusteeship Council was one of the principal organs of the UN."},
{"no":104,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nDistinguished Person\nAchievement / Known As\nA. Guenter Grass\n1. First woman Prime Minister of Canada\nB. Trevor Huddleston\n2. Nobel Prize Winner for Literature\nC. Dicky Dolma\n3. Leading campaigner against apartheid in South Africa\nD. Kim Campbell\n4. Youngest woman to climb Mt. Everest\n5. American violinist","A":"5 3 2 1","B":"2 3 4 1","C":"5 3 4 2","D":"2 4 1 3","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Personalities in News","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Guenter Grass → Nobel Prize Winner for Literature\nTrevor Huddleston → Anti-apartheid campaigner\nDicky Dolma → Youngest woman to climb Mt. Everest at the time\nKim Campbell → First woman Prime Minister of Canada\nHence the sequence is 2, 3, 4, 1.","revisionPoints":"Guenter Grass → Literature Nobel Prize.\nTrevor Huddleston → Anti-apartheid movement.\nDicky Dolma → Mount Everest.\nKim Campbell → Canadian Prime Minister."},
{"no":105,"text":"Which one of the following countries does not have a border with Lithuania?","A":"Poland","B":"Ukraine","C":"Belarus","D":"Latvia","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Lithuania shares land borders with:\nLatvia\nBelarus\nPoland\nRussia's Kaliningrad Oblast\nUkraine does not share a border with Lithuania.","revisionPoints":"Lithuania is one of the Baltic States.\nBaltic States:\nEstonia\nLatvia\nLithuania\nLithuania borders Latvia, Belarus, Poland and Russia (Kaliningrad)."},
{"no":106,"text":"Where are the Balearic Islands located?","A":"Mediterranean Sea","B":"Black Sea","C":"Baltic Sea","D":"North Sea","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The supplied source explicitly gives (b) as its answer.\nHowever, the source appears to contain a geographical error here: the Balearic Islands are actually located in the western Mediterranean Sea, off the eastern coast of Spain.\nTherefore, for study purposes, note the discrepancy rather than silently replacing the source answer.","revisionPoints":"Balearic Islands → Spain.\nThey are located in the Mediterranean Sea.\nMajor islands include Mallorca, Menorca, Ibiza and Formentera.\nSource key: (b), but the geographical fact is Mediterranean Sea."},
{"no":107,"text":"Which one of the following is the correct sequence of the given towns of Pakistan while moving from the North towards the South?","A":"Islamabad → Gujranwala → Peshawar → Multan","B":"Peshawar → Gujranwala → Multan → Islamabad","C":"Peshawar → Islamabad → Gujranwala → Multan","D":"Islamabad → Multan → Peshawar → Gujranwala","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Moving broadly from north toward south:\nPeshawar is the northernmost among these.\nIslamabad lies further south.\nGujranwala lies south-east of Islamabad.\nMultan lies further south.\nTherefore the sequence is Peshawar → Islamabad → Gujranwala → Multan.","revisionPoints":"Peshawar → Khyber Pakhtunkhwa.\nIslamabad → capital of Pakistan.\nGujranwala → Punjab.\nMultan → Punjab, further south.\nDirection-based map questions require approximate geographic positioning."},
{"no":108,"text":"Which pair is correctly matched?","A":"Bahamas — Nassau","B":"Costa Rica — San Jose","C":"Nicaragua — Belmopan","D":"Dominican Republic — Santo Domingo","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The supplied source gives (c) as the answer.\nHowever, this is another apparent error in the supplied source:\nNicaragua → Managua\nBelmopan → Belize\nCosta Rica → San José\nDominican Republic → Santo Domingo\nBahamas → Nassau\nThus, the source's answer is internally inconsistent with the geographical facts.","revisionPoints":"Nicaragua → Managua.\nBelize → Belmopan.\nCosta Rica → San José.\nDominican Republic → Santo Domingo.\nBahamas → Nassau.\nThis question should be flagged as a source error for revision."},
{"no":109,"text":"Consider the following sites/monuments:\n1. Champaner-Pavagadh Archaeological Park\n2. Chhatrapati Shivaji Railway Station, Mumbai\n3. Mamallapuram\n4. Sun Temple, Konark\nWhich of the above are included in the World Heritage List of UNESCO?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1, 3 and 4","C":"2 and 4","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"UNESCO Heritage","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"All four listed sites are associated with India's UNESCO World Heritage properties.\nTherefore the source identifies all four as correct.","revisionPoints":"Champaner-Pavagadh → Gujarat.\nChhatrapati Shivaji Terminus → Mumbai, Maharashtra.\nMamallapuram → Tamil Nadu.\nSun Temple, Konark → Odisha.\nUNESCO questions often test site + state + historical significance."},
{"no":110,"text":"Which one of the following airports in India was the first to be owned by a public limited company?","A":"Dabolim Airport, Goa","B":"Cochin Airport","C":"Hyderabad Airport","D":"Bangalore Airport","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Infrastructure","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Cochin Airport as the first airport in India developed/owned through a public limited company structure.\nCochin International Airport was developed through a company with participation from multiple stakeholders.","revisionPoints":"Cochin International Airport → Kerala.\nIt is associated with a distinctive public-participation ownership model.\nAirport-related questions can test location, ownership and development model."},
{"no":111,"text":"Which one of the following was probed by the Liberhan Commission?","A":"Test cricket match fixing","B":"Best Bakery Case","C":"Tehelka Tapes Case","D":"Demolition of the disputed structure at Ayodhya","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Commissions of Inquiry","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The Liberhan Commission was constituted to inquire into the circumstances surrounding the 6 December 1992 demolition at Ayodhya.\nIt examined events and the sequence leading to the demolition.","revisionPoints":"Liberhan Commission → Ayodhya demolition.\nCommission of Inquiry was headed by Justice M. S. Liberhan.\nDistinguish it from commissions dealing with other major controversies."},
{"no":112,"text":"Which one of the following is the correct statement?","A":"Modern Kochi was a Dutch colony till India's independence.","B":"The Dutch defeated the Portuguese and built Fort Williams in modern Kochi.","C":"Modern Kochi was first a Dutch colony before the Portuguese took over from them.","D":"Modern Kochi never became a part of the British colony.","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Colonial History","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The supplied source gives (b) as the answer.\nHowever, the wording appears historically problematic: Fort William is associated with Kolkata, not Kochi.\nTherefore, this should be treated as a source/extraction error rather than memorized as a factual statement.","revisionPoints":"Kochi has a history involving Portuguese, Dutch and British influence.\nPortuguese influence in Kochi predates Dutch control.\nFort William → Kolkata, not Kochi.\nFlag this question for verification if using the source as a revision key."},
{"no":113,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nPerson\nOrganisation\nA. V. R. S. Natrajan\n1. Bharat Heavy Electricals Limited\nB. A. K. Puri\n2. Air India\nC. V. Thulasidas\n3. Maruti Udyog Limited\nD. Jagdish Kumar\n4. Bharat Earth Movers Limited\n5. Indian Space Research Organisation","A":"2 3 5 1","B":"4 1 2 3","C":"2 1 5 3","D":"4 3 2 1","ans":"B","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Personalities in News","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source maps:\nV. R. S. Natrajan → Bharat Earth Movers Limited\nA. K. Puri → Bharat Heavy Electricals Limited\nV. Thulasidas → Air India\nJagdish Kumar → Maruti Udyog Limited\nHence the sequence is 4, 1, 2, 3.","revisionPoints":"Match-the-following questions require identifying the complete sequence.\nBHEL → Bharat Heavy Electricals Limited.\nAir India → national aviation sector.\nBEML → heavy equipment/engineering sector."},
{"no":114,"text":"Which one of the following statements is not correct?","A":"Rourkela Steel Plant, the first integrated steel plant in the public sector of India, was set up with Soviet Union collaboration.","B":"Salem Steel Plant is a premier producer of stainless steel in India.","C":"Maharashtra Elektrosmelt Ltd is a subsidiary of the Steel Authority of India Ltd.","D":"Visakhapatnam Steel Plant is a unit of Rashtriya Ispat Nigam Ltd.","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Industry","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies (a) as the incorrect statement.\nThe important distinction is the foreign collaboration associated with Rourkela Steel Plant.\nThe source therefore treats the Soviet collaboration claim as incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Rourkela Steel Plant → Odisha.\nSalem Steel Plant → Tamil Nadu.\nVisakhapatnam Steel Plant → Andhra Pradesh.\nRashtriya Ispat Nigam Limited (RINL) is associated with Visakhapatnam Steel Plant."},
{"no":115,"text":"Which one of the following companies started a rural marketing network called 'e-Choupals'?","A":"ITC","B":"Dabur","C":"Procter & Gamble","D":"Hindustan Lever","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Agriculture & Allied","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"e-Choupal was an initiative associated with ITC.\nIt used information and communication technology to connect farmers with information and markets.\nThe initiative became an important example of digital intervention in rural markets.","revisionPoints":"e-Choupal → ITC.\nTarget group → rural/agricultural communities.\nUses ICT for market and agricultural information.\nRelevant to agriculture + technology + rural economy."},
{"no":116,"text":"For which one of the following is Satara well-known?","A":"Thermal power plant","B":"Wind energy plant","C":"Hydroelectric plant","D":"Nuclear power plant","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Energy","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source associates Satara in Maharashtra with wind energy.\nThe region has been known for wind-energy development.","revisionPoints":"Satara → Maharashtra.\nSatara is associated with wind energy.\nRenewable energy locations may depend on factors such as wind speed, terrain and climatic conditions.\nLocation-based energy questions are important in Geography and Environment."},
{"no":117,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Vigyan Rail is a science exhibition on wheels organized by the Council of Scientific and Industrial Research.\n2. Vigyan Prasar is an autonomous body under the Ministry of Human Resource Development.\n3. EDUSAT, ISRO's educational satellite, was launched from French Guiana in 2004.\nWhich of the above statements are true?","A":"1, 2 & 3","B":"1 & 2","C":"2 & 3","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science Policy","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies 1 and 2 as correct.\nStatement 3 is not accepted by the source.\nThis is a source-dependent question, and the supplied material does not provide further explanation for the rejection of Statement 3.","revisionPoints":"Vigyan Rail → science exhibition on wheels.\nVigyan Prasar → science communication/outreach.\nEDUSAT → educational applications of satellite technology.\nThe source's institutional descriptions should be treated as period-specific."},
{"no":118,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Article 301 pertains to the Right to Property.\n2. Right to Property is a legal right but not a fundamental right.\n3. Article 300A was inserted in the Constitution of India by the 44th Constitutional Amendment.\nWhich of the above statements are true?","A":"2 only","B":"2 & 3","C":"1 & 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: Article 301 concerns freedom of trade, commerce and intercourse, not Right to Property.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Right to Property is currently a constitutional/legal right, not a Fundamental Right.\nStatement 3 — The supplied source marks this as incorrect, although the statement corresponds to the commonly documented history of Article 300A and the 44th Amendment.\nTherefore, the source key gives 2 only, but this item contains a source-key inconsistency.","revisionPoints":"Article 300A → Right to Property.\nRight to Property ceased to be a Fundamental Right after the 44th Amendment.\nArticle 301 → freedom of trade, commerce and intercourse.\nRemember the distinction between Fundamental Rights and other constitutional/legal rights."},
{"no":119,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Parliament cannot enlarge the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court of India as its jurisdiction is limited to that conferred by the Constitution.\n2. The officers and servants of the Supreme Court and High Courts are appointed by the concerned Chief Justice and the administrative expenses are charged on the Consolidated Fund of India.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect according to the source: Parliament can enlarge the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court in certain areas as provided by the Constitution.\nStatement 2 — Correct: The Constitution contains provisions regarding appointment of court staff and financial charges.\nHence the source identifies 2 only.","revisionPoints":"Supreme Court jurisdiction includes:\nOriginal jurisdiction\nAppellate jurisdiction\nAdvisory jurisdiction\nWrit jurisdiction\nArticle 138 deals with enlargement of the jurisdiction of the Supreme Court by Parliament.\nJudicial independence is supported by constitutional safeguards concerning finances and administration."},
{"no":120,"text":"According to the Census 2001, which one of the following Indian States had the maximum population in India after Uttar Pradesh?","A":"West Bengal","B":"Maharashtra","C":"Bihar","D":"Tamil Nadu","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Census","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"According to Census 2001, Uttar Pradesh had the largest population among Indian states.\nMaharashtra had the second-largest population.\nTherefore, the answer is Maharashtra.","revisionPoints":"Census 2001 → Uttar Pradesh → highest population.\nMaharashtra → second highest population in Census 2001.\nCensus questions should always be interpreted with the specific census year mentioned.\nPopulation rankings can change over time, so avoid applying historical rankings automatically to current data."},
{"no":121,"text":"Itaipu Dam, built on the River Paraná, is one of the largest dams in the world. Which two countries have this as a joint project?","A":"Brazil and Peru","B":"Paraguay and Ecuador","C":"Brazil and Paraguay","D":"Colombia and Paraguay","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Itaipu Dam is constructed on the Paraná River.\nIt is a joint project of Brazil and Paraguay.\nThe dam is associated with one of the world's major hydroelectric power projects.","revisionPoints":"Itaipu Dam → Brazil + Paraguay.\nRiver → Paraná.\nIt is a major hydroelectric power project.\nInternational river projects often involve cooperation between neighbouring countries."},
{"no":122,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. There are 25 High Courts in India.\n2. Punjab, Haryana and the Union Territory of Chandigarh have a common High Court.\n3. National Capital Territory of Delhi has a High Court of its own.\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?","A":"2 and 3","B":"1 and 2","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 2 — Correct: Punjab, Haryana and Chandigarh share the Punjab and Haryana High Court.\nStatement 3 — Correct: Delhi has its own High Court.\nStatement 1 — Incorrect according to the source: The number stated is not accepted.\nHence, 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"High Courts are constitutional courts at the State/Union Territory level.\nPunjab + Haryana + Chandigarh → common High Court.\nDelhi → Delhi High Court.\nThe number of High Courts is a time-sensitive fact and should be linked to the relevant year of the question."},
{"no":123,"text":"Which one of the following cities is not a former capital of the given country?","A":"Karachi — Pakistan","B":"Auckland — New Zealand","C":"Kyoto — Japan","D":"Brisbane — Australia","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Karachi was the first capital of Pakistan after independence.\nAuckland was the first capital of New Zealand.\nKyoto was the imperial capital of Japan for centuries.\nBrisbane has not been the national capital of Australia.\nAustralia's national capital is Canberra.","revisionPoints":"Pakistan → Karachi → former capital.\nNew Zealand → Auckland → former capital.\nJapan → Kyoto → former imperial capital.\nAustralia → Canberra → national capital."},
{"no":124,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Total land area of Earth is approximately 1475 lakh square kilometres.\n2. Ratio of land area to water area of Earth is approximately 1:4.\n3. Maximum percentage of Earth's water is in the Pacific Ocean.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 3","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 only","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect according to the source: The stated land area is not accepted.\nStatement 2 — Correct: Earth's surface has approximately one-fourth land and three-fourths water, giving a land-to-water ratio close to 1:4.\nStatement 3 — Correct: The Pacific Ocean contains the largest share of oceanic water by area/volume among the oceans.\nTherefore, 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"About 71% of Earth's surface is covered by water.\nAbout 29% is land.\nPacific Ocean → largest ocean.\nLand–water distribution is an important basic Geography concept."},
{"no":125,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Kyoto Protocol came into force in the year 2005.\n2. Kyoto Protocol deals primarily with depletion of the ozone layer.\n3. Methane as a greenhouse gas is more harmful than carbon dioxide.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"1 and 3","C":"1 only","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: The Kyoto Protocol entered into force in 2005.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: Kyoto Protocol primarily concerns greenhouse-gas emissions and climate change, not ozone-layer depletion.\nStatement 3 — Accepted by the source: Methane has a higher global-warming potential per unit mass than carbon dioxide over standard assessment periods.\nHence the source gives 1 and 3.","revisionPoints":"Kyoto Protocol → climate change / greenhouse gases.\nAdopted in 1997.\nEntered into force in 2005.\nCFCs and ozone depletion → Montreal Protocol, not Kyoto Protocol.\nMethane has a higher warming potential per molecule/mass than CO₂, although CO₂ is much more abundant and persistent."},
{"no":126,"text":"Which one of the following pairs is not correctly matched?","A":"Seikan Rail Tunnel — China","B":"Petronas Tower — Malaysia","C":"Appalachian Trail — United States of America","D":"Rogun Dam — Tajikistan","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Transport & Infrastructure","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Seikan Rail Tunnel is in Japan, not China.\nPetronas Towers → Malaysia.\nAppalachian Trail → United States.\nRogun Dam → Tajikistan.\nTherefore, option (a) is incorrectly matched.","revisionPoints":"Seikan Tunnel → Japan.\nPetronas Towers → Kuala Lumpur, Malaysia.\nAppalachian Trail → USA.\nRogun Dam → Tajikistan.\nInfrastructure-location matching is a recurring Geography/GK theme."},
{"no":127,"text":"Which one of the following is the correct sequence of the given continents in decreasing order of their percentage of Earth's land?","A":"North America → Africa → South America → Europe","B":"Africa → North America → South America → Europe","C":"North America → Africa → Europe → South America","D":"Africa → North America → Europe → South America","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Among the listed continents:\nAfrica has the largest land area.\nNorth America comes next.\nSouth America follows.\nEurope has the smallest area among these.\nHence the decreasing order is Africa → North America → South America → Europe.","revisionPoints":"Approximate area ranking:\nAsia\nAfrica\nNorth America\nSouth America\nAntarctica\nEurope\nAustralia\nAfrica → second-largest continent.\nEurope → relatively small land area."},
{"no":128,"text":"Match the countries with the names of their Parliaments:\nCountry\nParliament\nA. Netherlands\n1. Diet\nB. Ukraine\n2. States General\nC. Poland\n3. Supreme Council\nD. Japan\n4. Sejm","A":"4 1 2 3","B":"2 3 4 1","C":"4 3 2 1","D":"2 1 4 3","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"World Parliaments","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Netherlands → States General\nUkraine → Supreme Council / Verkhovna Rada\nPoland → Sejm\nJapan → Diet\nTherefore, the sequence is 2, 3, 4, 1.","revisionPoints":"Netherlands → States General.\nJapan → National Diet.\nPoland → Sejm and Senate.\nParliament names are common topics in International Relations/GK."},
{"no":129,"text":"For which one of the following countries is Spanish not an official language?","A":"Chile","B":"Colombia","C":"Republic of Congo","D":"Cuba","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Chile → Spanish\nColombia → Spanish\nCuba → Spanish\nThe Republic of Congo does not have Spanish as its official language; French is its official language.\nHence, option (c) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Chile → Spanish.\nColombia → Spanish.\nCuba → Spanish.\nRepublic of Congo → French.\nDo not confuse Republic of Congo with Democratic Republic of Congo; both primarily use French officially."},
{"no":130,"text":"Who among the following is not a recipient of the Bharat Ratna Award?","A":"Ustad Bismillah Khan","B":"Satyajit Ray","C":"Lata Mangeshkar","D":"Raj Kapoor","ans":"D","category":"Current Affairs","subcategory":"Awards & Honours","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Ustad Bismillah Khan received the Bharat Ratna.\nSatyajit Ray received the Bharat Ratna.\nLata Mangeshkar received the Bharat Ratna.\nRaj Kapoor was not a Bharat Ratna recipient.\nTherefore, option (d) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Bharat Ratna → India's highest civilian award.\nBismillah Khan → Shehnai.\nSatyajit Ray → Cinema.\nLata Mangeshkar → Playback singing/music.\nAward questions should be studied with the recipient's field of contribution."},
{"no":131,"text":"Match List-I with List-II:\nNational Park / Wildlife Sanctuary\nState\nA. Bondla Wildlife Sanctuary\n1. Odisha\nB. Kangerghat National Park\n2. Assam\nC. Orang Sanctuary\n3. Chhattisgarh\nD. Ushakothi Wildlife Sanctuary\n4. Goa\n5. Tripura","A":"2 1 5 3","B":"4 3 2 1","C":"2 3 5 1","D":"4 1 2 3","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"National Parks & Sanctuaries","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Bondla Wildlife Sanctuary → Goa\nKangerghat National Park → Chhattisgarh\nOrang Sanctuary → Assam\nUshakothi Wildlife Sanctuary → Odisha\nTherefore, the sequence is 4, 3, 2, 1.","revisionPoints":"Bondla → Goa.\nKangerghat/Kanger Valley → Chhattisgarh.\nOrang → Assam.\nUshakothi → Odisha.\nProtected-area questions are best remembered through State + ecological region."},
{"no":132,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Disputes with mobile cellular companies\n2. Motor accident cases\n3. Pension cases\nFor which of the above are Lok Adalats held?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies motor accident cases as the applicable category.\nLok Adalats are mechanisms for speedy settlement of certain disputes through compromise.\nThe source does not include the other listed categories for this question.","revisionPoints":"Lok Adalat → alternative dispute resolution mechanism.\nIt focuses on conciliation and compromise.\nIt can deal with pending cases as well as certain pre-litigation disputes.\nAwards of Lok Adalats have legal effect as provided by law."},
{"no":133,"text":"Which one of the following is not an ASEAN member?","A":"Cambodia","B":"China","C":"Laos","D":"Philippines","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Organizations","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Cambodia, Laos and the Philippines are ASEAN members.\nChina is not an ASEAN member.\nChina is instead an important ASEAN dialogue/partner country.","revisionPoints":"ASEAN → Association of Southeast Asian Nations.\nASEAN was established in 1967.\nFounding members included Indonesia, Malaysia, Philippines, Singapore and Thailand.\nChina → not an ASEAN member."},
{"no":134,"text":"Recently, to which of the following countries did India offer to build a Buddha Temple?","A":"China","B":"Myanmar","C":"Thailand","D":"Vietnam","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Bilateral Relations","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies China as the answer.\nThe question reflects India-China cultural and Buddhist heritage diplomacy during the period relevant to the 2005 paper.","revisionPoints":"Buddhism originated in the Indian subcontinent.\nIndia has historically maintained cultural links with several Buddhist-majority countries.\nBuddhist heritage is relevant to India's cultural diplomacy.\nCurrent-affairs questions must be interpreted according to the relevant year."},
{"no":135,"text":"Who among the following was a proponent of Fabianism as a movement?","A":"Annie Besant","B":"A. O. Hume","C":"Michael Madhusudan Dutt","D":"R. Palme Dutt","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Modern India","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Annie Besant as the answer.\nFabianism was associated with gradualist and evolutionary approaches to socialism.\nAnnie Besant was associated with social and political reform movements in Britain and India.","revisionPoints":"Fabianism → gradual/evolutionary socialism.\nFabian Society → founded in Britain in 1884.\nImportant associated figures include George Bernard Shaw and Sidney Webb.\nAnnie Besant later became an important Indian nationalist and Congress leader."},
{"no":136,"text":"What was Komagata Maru?","A":"A political party based in Taiwan","B":"Peasant communist leader of China","C":"A naval ship on voyage to Canada","D":"A Chinese village where Mao Tse Tung began his Long March","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Komagata Maru was a Japanese steamship involved in an important episode of Indian migration and anti-colonial history.\nIn 1914, it carried Indian passengers seeking entry into Canada.\nThe incident became an important part of the history of Indian overseas nationalism.","revisionPoints":"Komagata Maru incident → 1914.\nAssociated with Baba Gurdit Singh.\nThe passengers faced exclusionary immigration rules in Canada.\nThe episode is linked with the history of the Ghadar movement and Indian nationalism overseas."},
{"no":137,"text":"Where were the Ghadar revolutionaries, who became active during the outbreak of the First World War, based?","A":"Central America","B":"North America","C":"West America","D":"South America","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The Ghadar movement developed significantly among Indian migrants in North America.\nIts important centres included the United States and Canada.\nThe movement sought to promote revolutionary activity against British rule in India.","revisionPoints":"Ghadar Movement → North America.\nGhadar Party → 1913.\nImportant centre → San Francisco.\nIt became particularly active around the period of World War I.\nLala Hardayal was an important figure associated with the movement."},
{"no":138,"text":"On the eve of the launch of the Quit India Movement, Mahatma Gandhi:\n1. Asked government servants to resign.\n2. Asked soldiers to leave their posts.\n3. Asked the Princes of the Princely States to accept the sovereignty of their own people.\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Statement 3 only as correct.\nGandhi's position involved asking the rulers of princely states to recognize the sovereignty of their peoples.\nThe source does not accept Statements 1 and 2.","revisionPoints":"Quit India Movement → August 1942.\nAssociated with the call of “Do or Die”.\nThe movement followed the failure of the Cripps Mission.\nGandhi emphasized mass participation against colonial rule."},
{"no":139,"text":"In which one of the following provinces was a Congress ministry not formed under the Government of India Act, 1935?","A":"Bihar","B":"Madras","C":"Orissa","D":"Punjab","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Congress ministries were formed in several provinces following the 1937 elections.\nPunjab was not among the provinces where a Congress ministry was formed.\nTherefore, option (d) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Government of India Act, 1935 → provincial autonomy.\nProvincial elections were held in 1937.\nCongress formed ministries in several provinces.\nPunjab had a different political configuration during this period."},
{"no":140,"text":"At which Congress Session was the Working Committee authorized to launch a programme of Civil Disobedience?","A":"Bombay","B":"Lahore","C":"Lucknow","D":"Tripuri","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies the Lahore Congress Session as the answer.\nThe Lahore Session of 1929 is especially important in the history of the national movement.\nIt adopted the objective of Purna Swaraj.","revisionPoints":"Lahore Congress Session → 1929.\nPresident → Jawaharlal Nehru.\nPurna Swaraj became the Congress objective.\n26 January 1930 was observed as Independence Day.\nThe Civil Disobedience Movement was subsequently launched in 1930."},
{"no":141,"text":"In October 1920, who among the following headed a group of Indians gathered at Tashkent to set up a Communist Party of India?","A":"H. K. Sarkar","B":"P. C. Joshi","C":"M. C. Chagla","D":"M. N. Roy","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies M. N. Roy as the answer.\nA group of Indian revolutionaries gathered at Tashkent in 1920.\nThe initiative was connected with the early development of the communist movement among Indians.","revisionPoints":"M. N. Roy → Communist movement.\nTashkent → 1920.\nThe episode is associated with the early history of the Communist Party of India.\nM. N. Roy later became an important figure in international communist politics."},
{"no":142,"text":"Who among the following drafted the resolution on Fundamental Rights for the Karachi Session of the Congress in 1931?","A":"Dr. B. R. Ambedkar","B":"Pandit Jawaharlal Nehru","C":"Dr. Rajendra Prasad","D":"Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Jawaharlal Nehru as the person who drafted the resolution.\nThe Karachi Session of 1931 adopted an important resolution concerning Fundamental Rights and economic policy.\nThe resolution influenced the later constitutional framework.","revisionPoints":"Karachi Session → 1931.\nCongress President at Karachi → Vallabhbhai Patel.\nFundamental Rights resolution → important precursor to constitutional Fundamental Rights.\nThe resolution also addressed economic and social justice."},
{"no":143,"text":"Who among the following was not associated with the formation of the U.P. Kisan Sabha in February 1918?","A":"Indra Narain Dwivedi","B":"Gauri Shankar Misra","C":"Jawaharlal Nehru","D":"Madan Mohan Malviya","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Peasant Movements","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"The source identifies Jawaharlal Nehru as the answer.\nThe question concerns the early organization of the peasant movement in the United Provinces.\nThe other listed personalities are associated with the formation of the organization according to the source.","revisionPoints":"U.P. Kisan Sabha → 1918.\nAssociated with the development of organized peasant activity in the United Provinces.\nPeasant movements became increasingly important in the Indian national movement.\nDistinguish early Kisan Sabha organizations from later All India Kisan Sabha developments."},
{"no":144,"text":"Which pairs are correctly matched?\nMovement / Satyagraha\nPerson actively associated\nA. Champaran\nRajendra Prasad\nB. Ahmedabad Mill Workers\nMorarji Desai\nC. Kheda\nVallabhbhai Patel\nSelect the correct answer:","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"1 and 3","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Champaran → Rajendra Prasad: Correct according to the source.\nAhmedabad Mill Workers → Morarji Desai: Incorrect.\nKheda → Vallabhbhai Patel: Correct.\nTherefore, 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Champaran Satyagraha → 1917.\nKheda Satyagraha → 1918.\nAhmedabad Mill Strike → 1918.\nGandhi's early Indian movements focused on peasants, workers and local grievances.\nVallabhbhai Patel played an important role in the Kheda movement."},
{"no":145,"text":"Which one of the following places did Kunwar Singh, a prominent leader of the Revolt of 1857, belong to?","A":"Bihar","B":"Madhya Pradesh","C":"Rajasthan","D":"Uttar Pradesh","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Revolt of 1857","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Kunwar Singh was a prominent leader of the Revolt of 1857 from Bihar.\nHe was associated particularly with Jagdishpur in present-day Bihar.\nHe fought against British forces during the uprising.","revisionPoints":"Kunwar Singh → Bihar.\nMajor centre → Jagdishpur.\nRevolt of 1857 → major centres included Delhi, Kanpur, Lucknow, Jhansi and Bihar.\nKunwar Singh was one of the prominent regional leaders of the revolt."},
{"no":146,"text":"Which one of the following territories was not affected by the Revolt of 1857?","A":"Jhansi","B":"Chittor","C":"Jagdishpur","D":"Lucknow","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Revolt of 1857","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Jhansi was a major centre associated with Rani Lakshmibai.\nJagdishpur was associated with Kunwar Singh.\nLucknow was an important centre of the revolt in Awadh.\nThe source identifies Chittor as the territory not affected.","revisionPoints":"Jhansi → Rani Lakshmibai.\nJagdishpur → Kunwar Singh.\nLucknow/Awadh → major centre of the revolt.\nThe Revolt of 1857 was concentrated mainly in North and Central India."},
{"no":147,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Lord Mountbatten was the Viceroy when the Shimla Conference took place.\n2. The Indian Navy Revolt, 1946 took place when Indian sailors in the Royal Indian Navy at Bombay and Karachi rose against the Government.\nWhich of the statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"History","subcategory":"Freedom Movement","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Incorrect: The Shimla Conference of 1945 took place during Lord Wavell's tenure as Viceroy.\nStatement 2 — Correct: The Royal Indian Navy Mutiny/Revolt of 1946 involved Indian sailors, with Bombay and Karachi being important centres.\nHence, 2 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"Shimla Conference → 1945 → Lord Wavell.\nRoyal Indian Navy Revolt → 1946.\nImportant centres included Bombay and Karachi.\nThe revolt reflected growing unrest in the armed forces during the final phase of British rule."},
{"no":148,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Part IX of the Constitution of India contains provisions for Panchayats and was inserted by the Constitution (73rd Amendment) Act, 1992.\n2. Part IX-A of the Constitution of India contains provisions for municipalities and Article 243Q envisages two types of municipalities — a Municipal Council and a Municipal Corporation — for every State.\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Local Government","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct: Part IX deals with Panchayats and was inserted by the 73rd Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: Article 243Q provides for three categories of municipalities:\nNagar Panchayat\nMunicipal Council\nMunicipal Corporation\nTherefore, only Statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"73rd Amendment → Panchayati Raj → Part IX.\n74th Amendment → Municipalities → Part IX-A.\nArticle 243Q → three types of municipalities.\nNagar Panchayat → transitional area.\nMunicipal Council → smaller urban area.\nMunicipal Corporation → larger urban area."},
{"no":149,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Articles 371A to 371I were inserted in the Constitution of India to meet regional demands of Nagaland, Assam, Manipur, Andhra Pradesh, Sikkim, Mizoram, Arunachal Pradesh and Goa.\n2. The Constitutions of India and the United States of America envisage a dual polity (Union and States) but a single citizenship.\n3. A naturalized citizen of India can never be deprived of his citizenship.\nWhich of the above statements is/are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 3","C":"3 only","D":"1 only","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Statement 1 — Correct according to the source: The provisions under Article 371 and related articles address special arrangements for particular States.\nStatement 2 — Incorrect: The United States has dual citizenship, while India provides for single citizenship.\nStatement 3 — Incorrect: Indian citizenship can be terminated or deprived under the applicable legal provisions, including in specified circumstances.\nHence, 1 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"Article 371 series → special provisions for particular States.\nIndia follows single citizenship.\nUSA follows a system involving dual citizenship at federal/state levels.\nIndian citizenship can be lost through:\nRenunciation\nTermination\nDeprivation"},
{"no":150,"text":"Who among the following invented the laser?","A":"Theodore Maiman","B":"Denis Papin","C":"William Morton","D":"Francis Crick","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Science History","tags":["UPSC","2005"],"expl":"Theodore H. Maiman demonstrated the first working laser in 1960.\nHis device was a ruby laser.\nThe laser works through the principle of stimulated emission of radiation.","revisionPoints":"Laser → Theodore Maiman → 1960.\nLASER = Light Amplification by Stimulated Emission of Radiation.\nMaiman's first working laser used a ruby crystal.\nApplications include:\nMedicine\nTelecommunications\nIndustry\nMeasurement\nOptical technologies\nTop of Form\nBottom of Form\nTop of Form\nBottom of Form"},
];
